// Class 11 Physics Unit II: Kinematics (09 Marks)
// Motion in a Straight Line & Motion in a Plane (JKBOSE / NCERT Official Syllabus Alignment)
// Gold Standard Reference Textbook & 3-Tab Architecture (Pradeep's / S.L. Arora Standard)

export const c11Phy2HtmlOverview = "<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.75; font-size: 16px;\">\n\n  <!-- QUICK GLOSSARY & BASIC DEFINITIONS -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #8E2DE2; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">📖 Quick Glossary & Basic Definitions</h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #CBD5E1; margin: 0 0 16px 0; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center !important;\">Essential Foundational Concepts &bull; Unit II: Kinematics (09 Marks)</p>\n\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 12px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">1. Frame of Reference:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">A coordinate coordinate system attached to an observer along with a clock, relative to which the position and motion of a body are measured.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">2. Instantaneous Velocity:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">The time rate of change of position at a specific instant of time, defined mathematically as the derivative v = lim(&Delta;t&rarr;0) &Delta;x/&Delta;t = dx/dt.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">3. Uniform Acceleration:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Motion in which velocity changes by equal numerical amounts in equal intervals of time, however small these intervals may be (a = dv/dt = constant).</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">4. Position & Displacement Vectors:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Position vector r points from coordinate origin to point (x, y); displacement vector &Delta;r = r<sub>2</sub> - r<sub>1</sub> is the straight directed change in position.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">5. Unit Vector (Â):</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">A vector of unit magnitude (|Â| = 1) pointing purely in the direction of vector A: Â = A / |A|, carrying zero physical units and dimensions.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">6. Scalar & Vector Products:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Dot product A &bull; B = AB cos &theta; (scalar energy/work); Cross product A &times; B = AB sin &theta; n̂ (axial vector torque/rotational area).</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">7. Projectile Motion:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Two-dimensional curvilinear motion under constant vertical downward gravitational acceleration (g) and zero horizontal acceleration.</span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">8. Centripetal Acceleration:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">Inward radial acceleration a<sub>c</sub> = v<sup>2</sup>/r = &omega;<sup>2</sup>r responsible solely for continuously changing the direction of circular velocity.</span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- PART 1: MOTION IN A STRAIGHT LINE -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.1); border-left: 6px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 30px 0 20px 0; border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #FFFFFF; margin: 0; font-size: 22px; font-weight: bold;\">\n      PART 1 &bull; MOTION IN A STRAIGHT LINE (RECTILINEAR KINEMATICS)\n    </h2>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 1 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; border-bottom: 2px solid #8E2DE2; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 25px;\">\n    1. Frame of Reference & Motion in a Straight Line\n  </h2>\n\n  <p><b>Kinematics</b> is the foundational branch of classical mechanics that investigates and describes the motion of physical bodies without reference to the forces causing that motion. A physical object is described as being in a state of <b>rest</b> if its spatial position coordinates relative to a designated observer remain completely invariant as time elapses. Conversely, a body is in a state of <b>motion</b> if its position coordinates change continuously with time.</p>\n\n  <p>Crucially, rest and motion are not absolute states; they are purely <b>relative</b> concepts. A passenger seated inside a smoothly cruising high-speed train is at rest relative to fellow passengers and the train carriage, but simultaneously moving at 150 km/h relative to an observer standing beside the railway track on Earth. Furthermore, the Earth itself is revolving around the Sun at ~30 km/s, making absolute rest physically nonexistent.</p>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #8E2DE2; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #8E2DE2; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Frame of Reference</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">A designated coordinate framework (typically a 3D Cartesian coordinate system with mutually perpendicular X, Y, and Z axes) linked to an observer and fitted with a synchronized timekeeping device (clock), relative to which the position, displacement, velocity, and acceleration of a moving body are observed and mathematically recorded.</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b style=\"color: #8E2DE2;\">SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">None (Mathematical Reference System)</span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b style=\"color: #8E2DE2;\">Dimensions:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">None</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) Inertial vs Non-Inertial Frames of Reference</h3>\n  <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.85;\">\n    <li><b style=\"color: #8E2DE2;\">Inertial Frame of Reference:</b> A frame of reference in which Newton's First Law of Motion (Law of Inertia) holds strictly valid without fictitious forces. It is either completely stationary or moving with a uniform, non-accelerating velocity in a straight line (acceleration a<sub>frame</sub> = 0).</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #8E2DE2;\">Non-Inertial (Accelerated) Frame of Reference:</b> A frame that is accelerating linearly or rotating relative to an inertial frame (a<sub>frame</sub> &ne; 0). In a non-inertial frame, Newton's laws can only be applied by introducing fictitious (pseudo) forces, such as the centrifugal force or Coriolis force.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin-top: 20px;\">(ii) Dimensional Classification of Motion</h3>\n  <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.85;\">\n    <li><b style=\"color: #8E2DE2;\">One-Dimensional Motion (Rectilinear Motion):</b> Motion of an object along a single straight line, where only one coordinate (e.g. x) changes with time (e.g. a car on a straight highway, a freely falling stone under gravity).</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #8E2DE2;\">Two-Dimensional Motion (Motion in a Plane):</b> Motion where two coordinates (e.g. x and y) change simultaneously (e.g. a projectile fired at an angle, circular motion of a satellite, a billiard ball rolling across a table).</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #8E2DE2;\">Three-Dimensional Motion (Motion in Space):</b> Motion where all three spatial coordinates (x, y, z) change concurrently (e.g. a flying kite, a meandering bird, random thermal motion of gas molecules).</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 2 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; border-bottom: 2px solid #8E2DE2; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    2. Elementary Concepts of Differentiation and Integration for Describing Motion\n  </h2>\n\n  <p>Classical kinematics relies fundamentally on the mathematical language of infinitesimal calculus developed by Sir Isaac Newton and Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz. Because physical velocities and accelerations change continuously from instant to instant, discrete algebraic ratios are inadequate.</p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) Differentiation: The Instantaneous Rate of Change</h3>\n  <p>The derivative represents the limiting value of the average rate of change as the elapsed time interval &Delta;t shrinks infinitesimally toward zero:</p>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16.5px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\">\n    <b>v(t) = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dx</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> = lim_{Delta t \to 0} <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&Delta;x</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&Delta;t</span></span> &nbsp;&bull;&nbsp; a(t) = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dv</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d<sup>2</sup>x</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt<sup>2</sup></span></span> = v <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dv</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dx</span></span></b>\n  </div>\n\n  <p><b>Key Differentiation Formulas for Kinematics:</b></p>\n  <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.85;\">\n    <li>Power Rule: <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span>(t<sup>n</sup>) = n t<sup>n-1</sup> (e.g., if x = 3t<sup>2</sup>, then v = dx/dt = 6t).</li>\n    <li>Constant Rule: <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span>(C) = 0 (velocity of a stationary body is zero).</li>\n    <li>Chain Rule: a = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dv</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dv</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dx</span></span> &times; <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dx</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> = mathbf{v <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dv</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dx</span></span>} (vital when acceleration is given as a function of position).</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin-top: 20px;\">(ii) Integration: The Continuous Summation & Area Under Curves</h3>\n  <p>Integration is the inverse mathematical operation of differentiation. Physically, integration accumulates the continuous infinitesimal displacements or velocity increments over an extended time interval:</p>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16.5px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\">\n    <b>Delta x = x<sub>2</sub> - x<sub>1</sub> = &int;<sub>t<sub>1</sub></sub><sup>t<sub>2</sub></sup> v(t) , dt &nbsp;&bull;&nbsp; Delta v = v<sub>2</sub> - v<sub>1</sub> = &int;<sub>t<sub>1</sub></sub><sup>t<sub>2</sub></sup> a(t) , dt</b>\n  </div>\n\n  <p><b>Key Integration Formulas for Kinematics:</b></p>\n  <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.85;\">\n    <li>Power Rule: &int; t<sup>n</sup> dt = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">t<sup>n+1</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">n + 1</span></span> + C quad (n &ne; -1).</li>\n    <li>Definite Integral: &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>t</sup> t dt = [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">t<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>]<sub>0</sub><sup>t</sup> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> t<sup>2</sup>.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 3 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; border-bottom: 2px solid #8E2DE2; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    3. Uniform & Non-Uniform Motion, Instantaneous Velocity & Speed\n  </h2>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) Distance vs Displacement</h3>\n  <div style=\"overflow-x: auto; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); font-size: 14.5px;\">\n      <thead>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 2px solid #8E2DE2;\">\n          <th style=\"padding: 10px; text-align: left; color: #8E2DE2;\">Characteristic</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 10px; text-align: left; color: #8E2DE2;\">Distance (Path Length)</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 10px; text-align: left; color: #8E2DE2;\">Displacement (&Delta;x or &Delta;r)</th>\n        </tr>\n      </thead>\n      <tbody>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px; font-weight: bold; color: #FFFFFF;\">Physical Nature</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px;\">Scalar quantity. Total actual length of path traversed between initial and final points.</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px;\">Vector quantity. Shortest straight directed distance pointing from initial to final position.</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px; font-weight: bold; color: #FFFFFF;\">Sign & Magnitude</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px;\">Always positive (or zero for a body at rest). Distance &ge; 0.</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px;\">Can be positive, negative, or zero depending on choice of coordinate direction.</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px; font-weight: bold; color: #FFFFFF;\">Closed Loop Path</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px;\">Non-zero (e.g., in circular lap of radius r, distance = 2&pi;r).</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px;\">Strictly zero when body returns to its starting point (&Delta;x = 0).</td>\n        </tr>\n      </tbody>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16px; color: #76FF03;\">\n    <b>Fundamental Invariance Inequality: &nbsp; \text{Distance} ge |\text{Displacement}| &nbsp;&rArr;&nbsp; <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">\text{Distance}</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">|\text{Displacement}|</span></span> ge 1</b>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin-top: 20px;\">(ii) Average vs Instantaneous Velocity and Speed</h3>\n  <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.85;\">\n    <li><b style=\"color: #8E2DE2;\">Average Speed:</b> Total path length divided by total elapsed time: v<sub>avg</sub> = Total Distance / Total Time.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #8E2DE2;\">Average Velocity:</b> Net displacement vector divided by total elapsed time: <b>v</b><sub>avg</sub> = &Delta;<b>x</b> / &Delta;t.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #8E2DE2;\">Instantaneous Speed:</b> The absolute magnitude of instantaneous velocity at a specific point in time: |v(t)| = |dx/dt|. A vehicle's speedometer measures instantaneous speed.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 4 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; border-bottom: 2px solid #8E2DE2; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    4. Uniformly Accelerated Motion, Position-Time & Velocity-Time Graphs\n  </h2>\n\n  <p>Graphical representations provide instantaneous visual insights into kinematics. Two fundamental geometric theorems govern rectilinear graphs:</p>\n  <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.85;\">\n    <li><b style=\"color: #8E2DE2;\">Theorem 1 (Slope Theorem):</b> The slope (tangent of angle, m = tan &theta; = dy/dx) of a position-time graph yields <b>Instantaneous Velocity</b> (v = dx/dt). The slope of a velocity-time graph yields <b>Instantaneous Acceleration</b> (a = dv/dt).</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #8E2DE2;\">Theorem 2 (Area Theorem):</b> The area under a velocity-time graph bounded by the time axis represents <b>Net Displacement</b> (Delta x = int v dt). The area under an acceleration-time graph represents <b>Net Change in Velocity</b> (Delta v = int a dt).</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 5 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; border-bottom: 2px solid #8E2DE2; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    5. Relations for Uniformly Accelerated Motion (Graphical & Calculus Derivations)\n  </h2>\n\n  <p>For motion along a straight line under constant acceleration (a = \text{constant}), we derive the four master kinematic equations:</p>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.95); border: 1.5px solid rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.5); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin: 20px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FFD700; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 12px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;\">\n      <span>📐</span> FULL STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL PROOF: Derivation of v = u + a t (First Kinematic Equation)\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #8E2DE2;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 1: Definition of Acceleration</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">By definition, instantaneous acceleration is a = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dv</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span>.\n          <br>Separating variables: dv = a , dt.</div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #8E2DE2;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 2: Integration with Boundary Limits</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">At t = 0, velocity is the initial velocity u. At time t, velocity reaches final velocity v.\n          <br>&int;<sub>u</sub><sup>v</sup> dv = &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>t</sup> a , dt.\n          <br>Since acceleration a is constant, take it outside the integral:\n          <br>[v]<sub>u</sub><sup>v</sup> = a &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>t</sup> dt implies (v - u) = a [t]<sub>0</sub><sup>t</sup> = a (t - 0).</div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #8E2DE2;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 3: Final Equation</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\"><div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">v = u + a t</div></div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.95); border: 1.5px solid rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.5); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin: 20px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FFD700; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 12px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;\">\n      <span>📐</span> FULL STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL PROOF: Derivation of s = u t + ½ a t² (Second Kinematic Equation)\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #8E2DE2;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 1: Definition of Velocity</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">By definition, instantaneous velocity is v = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">ds</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span>.\n          <br>Separating variables: ds = v , dt.</div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #8E2DE2;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 2: Substitute First Equation of Motion</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Substitute v = u + at:\n          <br>ds = (u + at) , dt = u , dt + at , dt.</div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #8E2DE2;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 3: Integration Across Limits</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">At t = 0, position is initial position s = 0. At time t, position reaches displacement s.\n          <br>&int;<sub>0</sub><sup>s</sup> ds = &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>t</sup> u , dt + &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>t</sup> a t , dt.\n          <br>[s]<sub>0</sub><sup>s</sup> = u [t]<sub>0</sub><sup>t</sup> + a [<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">t<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>]<sub>0</sub><sup>t</sup>.</div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #8E2DE2;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 4: Final Equation</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\"><div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">s = u t + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> a t<sup>2</sup></div></div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.95); border: 1.5px solid rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.5); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin: 20px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FFD700; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 12px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;\">\n      <span>📐</span> FULL STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL PROOF: Derivation of v² - u² = 2 a s (Third Kinematic Equation)\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #8E2DE2;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 1: Chain Rule for Acceleration</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Express acceleration via chain rule eliminating explicit time variable t:\n          <br>a = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dv</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dv</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">ds</span></span> cdot <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">ds</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> = v <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dv</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">ds</span></span>.</div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #8E2DE2;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 2: Separate Variables</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Rearrange terms: v , dv = a , ds.</div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #8E2DE2;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 3: Integrate with Limits</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">At s = 0, velocity is u. At displacement s, velocity is v:\n          <br>&int;<sub>u</sub><sup>v</sup> v , dv = &int;<sub>0</sub><sup>s</sup> a , ds.\n          <br>[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>]<sub>u</sub><sup>v</sup> = a [s]<sub>0</sub><sup>s</sup> implies <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v<sup>2</sup> - u<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> = a s.</div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #8E2DE2;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 4: Final Equation</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\"><div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">v<sup>2</sup> - u<sup>2</sup> = 2 a s &nbsp;&rArr;&nbsp; v<sup>2</sup> = u<sup>2</sup> + 2 a s</div></div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.95); border: 1.5px solid rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.5); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin: 20px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FFD700; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 12px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;\">\n      <span>📐</span> FULL STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL PROOF: Distance Traversed in the n-th Second (s_n = u + a/2 (2n - 1))\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #8E2DE2;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 1: Conceptual Setup</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">The distance covered specifically in the n-th second is the difference between displacement after n seconds and displacement after (n-1) seconds:\n          <br>s_n = s(n) - s(n-1).</div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #8E2DE2;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 2: Algebraic Expansion</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">s(n) = u n + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> a n<sup>2</sup>\n          <br>s(n-1) = u (n - 1) + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> a (n - 1)<sup>2</sup> = u n - u + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> a (n<sup>2</sup> - 2n + 1).</div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #8E2DE2;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 3: Subtract Terms</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">s_n = left[u n + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> a n<sup>2</sup>\right] - left[u n - u + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> a n<sup>2</sup> - a n + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> a\right]\n          <br>s_n = u n + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> a n<sup>2</sup> - u n + u - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> a n<sup>2</sup> + a n - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> a\n          <br>s_n = u + a n - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> a = u + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> (2n - 1).</div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #8E2DE2;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 4: Final Equation</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\"><div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">s_n = u + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> (2n - 1)</div></div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n\n  <!-- PART 2: MOTION IN A PLANE -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.1); border-left: 6px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 40px 0 20px 0; border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #FFFFFF; margin: 0; font-size: 22px; font-weight: bold;\">\n      PART 2 &bull; MOTION IN A PLANE (VECTORS, 2D PROJECTILE & CIRCULAR MOTION)\n    </h2>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 6 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; border-bottom: 2px solid #8E2DE2; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 25px;\">\n    6. Scalar & Vector Quantities, Position & Displacement Vectors\n  </h2>\n\n  <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.85;\">\n    <li><b style=\"color: #8E2DE2;\">Scalar Quantities:</b> Physical quantities possessing magnitude and appropriate physical units, but completely devoid of spatial direction (e.g. Mass, Length, Time, Work, Energy, Temperature, Electric Current). They obey ordinary scalar algebra (2 \text{ kg} + 3 \text{ kg} = 5 \text{ kg}).</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #8E2DE2;\">Vector Quantities:</b> Physical quantities that possess magnitude, a definite spatial direction, and <b>must strictly obey the Triangle/Parallelogram Laws of Vector Addition</b> (e.g. Displacement, Velocity, Acceleration, Force, Momentum, Torque).</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(239, 68, 68, 0.08); border: 1.2px solid #EF4444; border-left: 5px solid #EF4444; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 16px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #F87171; font-size: 15px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">⚠️ BOARD EXAM FOCUS & COMMON MISCONCEPTION: Electric Current Has Direction But is a SCALAR!</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FEE2E2; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65;\">Electric current flows along a specific wire direction, yet it is strictly a <b>scalar</b> quantity. This is because electric current does NOT obey the vector laws of addition. Current entering a junction obeys Kirchhoff's Junction Rule (I_1 + I_2 = I_3) algebraically, completely independent of the spatial geometric angle between the wires.</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 7 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; border-bottom: 2px solid #8E2DE2; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    7. Vector Addition Laws & Analytical Formulation\n  </h2>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) Triangle Law of Vector Addition</h3>\n  <p>If two vectors \u000bec{A} and \u000bec{B} are represented in magnitude and direction by two consecutive sides of a triangle taken in the same cyclic order (head to tail), then their resultant \u000bec{R} = \u000bec{A} + \u000bec{B} is represented in magnitude and direction by the third closing side of the triangle taken in the reverse order (tail of first to head of second).</p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin-top: 20px;\">(ii) Parallelogram Law of Vector Addition (Analytical Proof)</h3>\n  <p>If two vectors \u000bec{P} and \u000bec{Q} acting simultaneously at a point are represented in magnitude and direction by the two adjacent sides of a parallelogram drawn from a common point, their resultant \u000bec{R} = \u000bec{P} + \u000bec{Q} is represented in magnitude and direction by the diagonal passing through that common intersection point.</p>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16.5px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\">\n    <b>R = sqrt{P<sup>2</sup> + Q<sup>2</sup> + 2 P Q cos \theta} &nbsp;&bull;&nbsp; \tan alpha = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">Q sin \theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">P + Q cos \theta</span></span></b>\n  </div>\n\n  <p><b>Special Geometric Boundary Cases:</b></p>\n  <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.85;\">\n    <li><b>Parallel Vectors (&theta; = 0&deg;):</b> cos 0<sup>c</sup>irc = 1 implies R = sqrt{P<sup>2</sup> + Q<sup>2</sup> + 2PQ} = mathbf{P + Q} (Maximum possible resultant magnitude, alpha = 0<sup>c</sup>irc).</li>\n    <li><b>Antiparallel Vectors (&theta; = 180&deg;):</b> cos 180<sup>c</sup>irc = -1 implies R = sqrt{P<sup>2</sup> + Q<sup>2</sup> - 2PQ} = mathbf{|P - Q|} (Minimum possible resultant magnitude).</li>\n    <li><b>Orthogonal Vectors (&theta; = 90&deg;):</b> cos 90<sup>c</sup>irc = 0 implies mathbf{R = sqrt{P<sup>2</sup> + Q<sup>2</sup>}} and \tan alpha = Q / P.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 8 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; border-bottom: 2px solid #8E2DE2; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    8. Unit Vectors & Rectangular Components in 2D and 3D\n  </h2>\n\n  <p>A <b>Unit Vector</b> hat{A} has a magnitude of exactly unity (|hat{A}| = 1) and indicates direction purely: mathbf{hat{A} = \u000bec{A} / |\u000bec{A}|}. In 3D Cartesian coordinates, orthogonal unit vectors along the X, Y, and Z axes are designated as mathbf{hat{i}, hat{j}, hat{k}} respectively.</p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) Resolution of a Vector in a Plane (2D)</h3>\n  <p>Any vector \u000bec{A} lying in the XY-plane making an angle \theta with the X-axis can be resolved into two mutually perpendicular rectangular components:</p>\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16.5px; color: #FFFFFF; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\">\n    <b>\u000bec{A} = A_x hat{i} + A_y hat{j} = (A cos \theta) hat{i} + (A sin \theta) hat{j} &nbsp;&bull;&nbsp; |\u000bec{A}| = sqrt{A_x<sup>2</sup> + A_y<sup>2</sup>} &nbsp;&bull;&nbsp; \tan \theta = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A_y</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A_x</span></span></b>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin-top: 20px;\">(ii) Resolution in 3D & Direction Cosines</h3>\n  <p>In 3D space: mathbf{\u000bec{A} = A_x hat{i} + A_y hat{j} + A_z hat{k}} with magnitude mathbf{|\u000bec{A}| = sqrt{A_x<sup>2</sup> + A_y<sup>2</sup> + A_z<sup>2</sup>}}. If \u000bec{A} makes angles alpha, \beta, gamma with the positive X, Y, and Z axes respectively, its <b>direction cosines</b> are l = cos alpha = A_x/A, m = cos \beta = A_y/A, n = cos gamma = A_z/A, satisfying the fundamental identity:</p>\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin: 8px 0;\">\n    cos<sup>2</sup> alpha + cos<sup>2</sup> \beta + cos<sup>2</sup> gamma = l<sup>2</sup> + m<sup>2</sup> + n<sup>2</sup> = 1\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 9 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; border-bottom: 2px solid #8E2DE2; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    9. Scalar (Dot) Product and Vector (Cross) Product\n  </h2>\n\n  <div style=\"overflow-x: auto; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.9); font-size: 14px;\">\n      <thead>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 2px solid #8E2DE2;\">\n          <th style=\"padding: 10px; text-align: left; color: #8E2DE2;\">Property</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 10px; text-align: left; color: #8E2DE2;\">Scalar (Dot) Product &bull; A &bull; B</th>\n          <th style=\"padding: 10px; text-align: left; color: #8E2DE2;\">Vector (Cross) Product &bull; A &times; B</th>\n        </tr>\n      </thead>\n      <tbody>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px; font-weight: bold; color: #FFFFFF;\">Mathematical Definition</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px;\">\u000bec{A} cdot \u000bec{B} = A B cos \theta (a pure scalar number).</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px;\">\u000bec{A} \times \u000bec{B} = (A B sin \theta) hat{n} (an axial vector normal to plane).</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px; font-weight: bold; color: #FFFFFF;\">Commutative Law</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px;\">Commutative: \u000bec{A} cdot \u000bec{B} = \u000bec{B} cdot \u000bec{A}.</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px;\">Anti-commutative: \u000bec{A} \times \u000bec{B} = -(\u000bec{B} \times \u000bec{A}).</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px; font-weight: bold; color: #FFFFFF;\">Self Product</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px;\">\u000bec{A} cdot \u000bec{A} = A<sup>2</sup> (square of magnitude).</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px;\">\u000bec{A} \times \u000bec{A} = \u000bec{0} (null vector).</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px; font-weight: bold; color: #FFFFFF;\">Orthogonal Vectors (&theta; = 90&deg;)</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px;\">\u000bec{A} cdot \u000bec{B} = 0 (Zero). Condition of orthogonality!</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px;\">|\u000bec{A} \times \u000bec{B}| = A B (Maximum magnitude).</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08);\">\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px; font-weight: bold; color: #FFFFFF;\">Unit Vectors Relations</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px;\">hat{i} cdot hat{i} = hat{j} cdot hat{j} = hat{k} cdot hat{k} = 1<br>hat{i} cdot hat{j} = hat{j} cdot hat{k} = hat{k} cdot hat{i} = 0.</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px;\">hat{i} \times hat{i} = hat{j} \times hat{j} = hat{k} \times hat{k} = \u000bec{0}<br>hat{i} \times hat{j} = hat{k}, ; hat{j} \times hat{k} = hat{i}, ; hat{k} \times hat{i} = hat{j}.</td>\n        </tr>\n        <tr>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px; font-weight: bold; color: #FFFFFF;\">Determinant Form in Components</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px;\">\u000bec{A} cdot \u000bec{B} = A_x B_x + A_y B_y + A_z B_z.</td>\n          <td style=\"padding: 10px;\">\u000bec{A} \times \u000bec{B} = \begin{vmatrix} hat{i} & hat{j} & hat{k} \\ A_x & A_y & A_z \\ B_x & B_y & B_z end{vmatrix}.</td>\n        </tr>\n      </tbody>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 10 & 11 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; border-bottom: 2px solid #8E2DE2; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    10 &bull; Projectile Motion in a Vertical Plane\n  </h2>\n\n  <p>A <b>projectile</b> is any body projected into space with an initial velocity \u000bec{u} that moves under the sole influence of gravity (neglecting air resistance). By Galileo's Principle of Independence of Perpendicular Motions, horizontal motion and vertical motion proceed simultaneously without affecting each other:</p>\n\n  <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.85;\">\n    <li><b style=\"color: #8E2DE2;\">Horizontal Motion (X-Axis):</b> Acceleration a_x = 0. Velocity remains constant: u_x = u cos \theta implies x = (u cos \theta) t.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #8E2DE2;\">Vertical Motion (Y-Axis):</b> Acceleration a_y = -g. Initial velocity u_y = u sin \theta implies y = (u sin \theta) t - \frac{1}{2} g t<sup>2</sup>.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.95); border: 1.5px solid rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.5); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin: 20px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #FFD700; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 12px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;\">\n      <span>📐</span> FULL STEP-BY-STEP MATHEMATICAL PROOF: Full Derivation of Projectile Motion Formulas (Trajectory, Time, Height, Range)\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #8E2DE2;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 1: Equation of Trajectory (Path of Projectile)</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">From horizontal motion: t = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">x</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u cos \theta</span></span>.\n          <br>Substitute t into vertical displacement equation:\n          <br>y = (u sin \theta) left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">x</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u cos \theta</span></span>\right) - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> g left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">x</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u cos \theta</span></span>\right)<sup>2</sup>\n          <br>y = x left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">sin \theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">cos \theta</span></span>\right) - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g x<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 u<sup>2</sup> cos<sup>2</sup> \theta</span></span> = mathbf{x \tan \theta - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g x<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 u<sup>2</sup> cos<sup>2</sup> \theta</span></span>}.\n          <br>This is in standard quadratic form y = Ax - Bx<sup>2</sup>, proving the trajectory is a <b>Parabola</b>.</div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #8E2DE2;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 2: Time of Flight (T)</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">At the end of flight, vertical displacement y = 0:\n          <br>0 = (u sin \theta) T - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> g T<sup>2</sup> implies T left(u sin \theta - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> g T\right) = 0.\n          <br>Since T \ne 0: <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> g T = u sin \theta implies mathbf{T = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 u sin \theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>}.\n          <br>(Time of ascent t_a = \frac{u sin \theta}{g} = \text{Time of descent } t_d).</div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #8E2DE2;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 3: Maximum Height Attained (H)</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">At maximum height peak, vertical velocity momentarily vanishes: v_y = 0.\n          <br>Using v_y<sup>2</sup> = u_y<sup>2</sup> - 2 g H:\n          <br>0 = (u sin \theta)<sup>2</sup> - 2 g H implies 2 g H = u<sup>2</sup> sin<sup>2</sup> \theta implies mathbf{H = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> sin<sup>2</sup> \theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 g</span></span>}.</div>\n    </div>\n  \n    <div style=\"margin-bottom: 12px; padding-left: 10px; border-left: 2.5px solid #8E2DE2;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; font-size: 14.5px; display: block; margin-bottom: 3px;\">Step 4: Horizontal Range (R) & Maximum Range Condition</b>\n      <div style=\"color: #E2E8F0; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6;\">Horizontal range is horizontal distance covered during total time of flight T:\n          <br>R = u_x \times T = (u cos \theta) \times left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 u sin \theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>\right) = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> (2 sin \theta cos \theta)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>.\n          <br>Using trigonometric identity 2 sin \theta cos \theta = sin 2\theta:\n          <div style=\"text-align: center; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16.5px; font-weight: bold; margin: 6px 0;\">\n            R = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> sin 2\theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>\n          </div>\n          &bull; <b>Maximum Range:</b> R is maximum when sin 2\theta = 1 implies 2\theta = 90<sup>c</sup>irc implies mathbf{\theta = 45<sup>c</sup>irc}.\n          <br>mathbf{R_{max} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span> = 2 H_{max}} (Maximum range is twice the maximum height!).<br>\n          &bull; <b>Two Angles of Projection for Same Range:</b> For a given speed u, the range is identical for complementary angles mathbf{\theta} and mathbf{(90<sup>c</sup>irc - \theta)} because sin(2(90<sup>c</sup>irc - \theta)) = sin(180<sup>c</sup>irc - 2\theta) = sin 2\theta.</div>\n    </div>\n  \n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION 12 -->\n  <h2 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; border-bottom: 2px solid #8E2DE2; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-top: 30px;\">\n    12. Uniform Circular Motion & Centripetal Acceleration\n  </h2>\n\n  <p>When a particle moves along a circular trajectory of radius r with constant linear speed v, its motion is termed <b>Uniform Circular Motion (UCM)</b>. Even though speed is constant, the velocity vector changes direction continuously at every infinitesimal point, making UCM a strictly <b>accelerated motion</b>.</p>\n\n  <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin-top: 20px;\">(i) Angular Kinematics Relations</h3>\n  <ul style=\"margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.85;\">\n    <li><b style=\"color: #8E2DE2;\">Angular Displacement (&theta;):</b> \theta = s / r \text{ (radians)}.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #8E2DE2;\">Angular Velocity (&omega;):</b> omega = lim_{Delta t \to 0} \frac{Delta \theta}{Delta t} = \frac{d\theta}{dt} = \frac{2pi}{T} = 2pi \nu \text{ (rad s}<sup>-1</sup>\text{)}.</li>\n    <li><b style=\"color: #8E2DE2;\">Relation Between Linear & Angular Velocity:</b> mathbf{v = r omega} quad (\u000bec{v} = \u000bec{omega} \times \u000bec{r}).</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.85); border: 1.2px solid rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.4); border-left: 5px solid #8E2DE2; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; margin: 18px 0;\">\n    <div style=\"color: #8E2DE2; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 4px;\">📖 DEFINITION: Centripetal Acceleration (a_c)</div>\n    <div style=\"color: #FFFFFF; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65;\">The radial acceleration experienced by a body executing circular motion, directed radially inward toward the instantaneous center of curvature, which acts exclusively to alter the direction of velocity without changing its magnitude.</div>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 13.5px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px; margin-top: 8px;\">\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b style=\"color: #8E2DE2;\">SI Unit:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">m s<sup>-2</sup></span></span>\n      <span style=\"color: #94A3B8;\"><b style=\"color: #8E2DE2;\">Dimensions:</b> <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF;\">[M<sup>0</sup> L<sup>1</sup> T<sup>-2</sup>]</span></span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3); border: 1px solid rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.35); border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; text-align: center; margin: 16px 0; font-size: 16.5px; color: #76FF03; white-space: nowrap; overflow-x: auto;\">\n    <b>a<sub>c</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span></span> = omega<sup>2</sup> r = 4pi<sup>2</sup> \nu<sup>2</sup> r = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4pi<sup>2</sup> r</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">T<sup>2</sup></span></span> &nbsp;&bull;&nbsp; \u000bec{a}_c = -omega<sup>2</sup> \u000bec{r}</b>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- MASTER REVISION FORMULA CHEAT SHEET -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #8E2DE2; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-top: 35px; margin-bottom: 15px;\">\n    <h2 class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">⚡ Master Revision Formula Cheat Sheet</h2>\n    <p class=\"text-center\" style=\"color: #CBD5E1; margin: 0 0 16px 0; font-size: 14.5px; text-align: center !important;\">Quick Reference Formulas & Kinematic Laws &bull; Unit II: Kinematics</p>\n\n    <div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); gap: 12px; font-size: 14px;\">\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Rectilinear Equations:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          v = u + a t<br>\n          s = u t + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> a t<sup>2</sup><br>\n          v<sup>2</sup> - u<sup>2</sup> = 2 a s<br>\n          s_n = u + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> (2n - 1)\n        </span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Vector Resultant & Direction:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          R = sqrt{P<sup>2</sup> + Q<sup>2</sup> + 2 P Q cos \theta}<br>\n          \tan alpha = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">Q sin \theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">P + Q cos \theta</span></span><br>\n          |\u000bec{A}| = sqrt{A_x<sup>2</sup> + A_y<sup>2</sup> + A_z<sup>2</sup>}\n        </span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Vector Products:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          \u000bec{A} cdot \u000bec{B} = A B cos \theta = A_x B_x + A_y B_y + A_z B_z<br>\n          \u000bec{A} \times \u000bec{B} = (A B sin \theta) hat{n}<br>\n          cos \theta = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">\u000bec{A} cdot \u000bec{B}</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A B</span></span>\n        </span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Projectile Trajectory & Time:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          y = x \tan \theta - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g x<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 u<sup>2</sup> cos<sup>2</sup> \theta</span></span><br>\n          Time of Flight: T = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 u sin \theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span><br>\n          Time to Peak: t_a = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u sin \theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>\n        </span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Projectile Height & Range:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          Max Height: H = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> sin<sup>2</sup> \theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 g</span></span><br>\n          Horizontal Range: R = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> sin 2\theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span><br>\n          R_{max} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span> quad (\theta = 45<sup>c</sup>irc)<br>\n          R(\theta) = R(90<sup>c</sup>irc - \theta)\n        </span>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.25); padding: 12px 14px; border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; border-radius: 6px;\">\n        <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 4px;\">&bull; Uniform Circular Motion:</b>\n        <span style=\"color: #FFFFFF; line-height: 1.6; display: block;\">\n          Linear Speed: v = r omega = 2pi r \nu = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2pi r</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">T</span></span><br>\n          Centripetal Accel: a_c = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span></span> = omega<sup>2</sup> r<br>\n          Tangential Accel: a_t = r alpha = r left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">domega</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span>\right)$\n        </span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n</div>";

export const c11Phy2HtmlSolutions = "<style>\n  p, li, div:not(.table-container):not(.table-responsive):not(.pt-scroll-wrapper):not(.mcq-option) {\n    text-align: justify !important;\n  }\n  h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6 { text-align: left; }\n  .text-center { text-align: center !important; }\n  .text-left { text-align: left !important; }\n</style>\n\n<div style=\"padding: 12px; color: #E0E0E0; text-align: justify; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 16px;\">\n\n  <!-- TITLE BANNER -->\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border: 1.5px solid #8E2DE2; border-radius: 12px; padding: 18px; margin-bottom: 25px; text-align: center;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 6px 0; font-size: 22px; font-weight: bold; text-align: center !important;\">\n      NCERT & Master Solutions Guide (100 Questions)\n    </h2>\n    <p style=\"color: #CBD5E1; margin: 0; font-size: 15px; text-align: center !important;\">\n      Class 11 Physics Unit II: Kinematics &bull; Motion in a Straight Line & Motion in a Plane (09 Marks)\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION A: VSA QUESTIONS -->\n  <div style=\"margin-top: 25px; margin-bottom: 15px; border-bottom: 2px solid #8E2DE2; padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold;\">\n      Section A: Very Short Answer (VSA) Questions (1 Mark each) &bull; Q1 to Q40\n    </h2>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; margin: 4px 0 0 0; font-size: 14px;\">\n      Fundamental definitions, 1-line physical reasoning, instantaneous calculus concepts, vector types, and graphical slopes.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q1: Explain why rest and motion are termed relative concepts.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      An object is at rest or in motion only relative to a chosen observer or frame of reference. A person seated in a moving bus is at rest relative to other passengers, but simultaneously in motion relative to an observer on the roadside. There is no absolute rest in the universe.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q2: Define a Frame of Reference.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      A frame of reference is a coordinate system (such as Cartesian axes X, Y, Z) attached to an observer and equipped with a synchronized clock, relative to which the position and motion of physical bodies are measured.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q3: Differentiate between an inertial frame and a non-inertial frame of reference.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      An <b>inertial frame</b> is either stationary or moving with constant velocity (zero acceleration), where Newton's laws hold valid without fictitious forces. A <b>non-inertial frame</b> is accelerating or rotating, requiring fictitious (pseudo) forces to apply Newton's laws.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q4: State the mathematical inequality relating Distance and Displacement.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      Distance is the total path length, while displacement is the shortest straight-line directed distance. Therefore: <b>Distance &ge; |Displacement|</b>, or <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">Distance</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">|Displacement|</span></span> &ge; 1.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q5: Under what condition is the magnitude of displacement equal to the distance covered?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The magnitude of displacement equals distance if and only if the particle moves strictly along a <b>straight line in a fixed direction without reversing its motion</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q6: Can the displacement of a moving body be zero even when its distance traveled is non-zero?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      <b>Yes.</b> Whenever a moving body returns to its initial starting position (such as completing one full circular lap of radius r, where distance = 2&pi;r), its initial and final coordinates coincide, making <b>displacement = 0</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q7: Define Instantaneous Velocity and write its mathematical formula.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      Instantaneous velocity is the time rate of change of position at a specific instant of time. Mathematically, it is the first derivative of position with respect to time: <b>v = lim(&Delta;t &rarr; 0) <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&Delta;x</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&Delta;t</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dx</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q8: What does the speedometer of an automobile indicate?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The speedometer of an automobile measures the <b>instantaneous speed</b> of the vehicle at that precise moment in time, expressed in km/h or m/s.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q9: Can a body possess zero instantaneous velocity and simultaneously non-zero acceleration? Give an example.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      <b>Yes.</b> When a body is projected vertically upwards under gravity, at its <b>highest point</b> its instantaneous velocity is momentarily zero (v = 0), but its downward acceleration due to gravity is non-zero: a = g = 9.8 m s<sup>-2</sup>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q10: Can a body have a constant speed and still have a varying velocity? Explain.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      <b>Yes.</b> In <b>Uniform Circular Motion</b>, the speed remains constant, but the direction of velocity changes continuously at every point along the circular path, meaning the velocity vector is continuously varying.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q11: Can a body have a constant velocity and a varying speed? Explain.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      <b>No.</b> Velocity is a vector composed of magnitude (speed) and direction. If velocity is constant, its magnitude (speed) must strictly be constant.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q12: What physical quantity is represented by the slope of a position-time (x - t) graph?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The slope (dx/dt) of a position-time graph represents the <b>Instantaneous Velocity</b> of the body.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q13: What physical quantity is represented by the slope of a velocity-time (v - t) graph?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The slope (dv/dt) of a velocity-time graph represents the <b>Instantaneous Acceleration</b> of the body.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q14: What physical quantity is given by the area under a velocity-time (v - t) graph?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The area under a velocity-time curve bounded by the time axis represents the <b>Net Displacement</b> of the particle (&Delta;x = &int; v dt).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q15: What physical quantity is given by the area under an acceleration-time (a - t) graph?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The area under an acceleration-time graph bounded by the time axis represents the <b>Net Change in Velocity</b> of the body (&Delta;v = &int; a dt).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q16: What is negative acceleration called, and when does it occur?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      Negative acceleration is termed <b>retardation</b> or <b>deceleration</b>. It occurs whenever the acceleration vector is directed opposite to the direction of velocity, causing speed to decrease.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q17: Write the formula for the distance traveled by a uniformly accelerated body in the n-th second.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The distance traveled in the n-th second is: <b>s<sub>n</sub> = u + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> (2n - 1)</b>, where u is initial velocity and a is uniform acceleration.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q18: Why is electric current treated as a scalar quantity despite possessing direction?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      Electric current does not obey the vector laws of addition (triangle/parallelogram law). Current divides and combines at junctions by simple algebraic addition (Kirchhoff's rule), independent of the angle between wires.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q19: Define a Unit Vector and write its mathematical expression.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      A unit vector is a vector having a magnitude of exactly unity (|Â| = 1) pointing in the direction of vector A. It is dimensionless and unitless: <b>Â = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">|A|</span></span></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q20: What are the orthogonal unit vectors along the Cartesian X, Y, and Z axes?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The orthogonal unit vectors are <b>î</b> along positive X-axis, <b>ĵ</b> along positive Y-axis, and <b>k̂</b> along positive Z-axis.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q21: Define a Null (Zero) Vector. What is its physical significance?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      A null vector is a vector having zero magnitude and an indeterminate direction: <b>0</b>. It is physically necessary to define operations such as subtracting a vector from itself (A - A = 0) or the cross product of parallel vectors.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q22: State the condition for two non-zero vectors A and B to be collinear.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      Two vectors are collinear if they act along the same line or parallel lines. Mathematically, <b>A = &lambda; B</b>, where &lambda; is a scalar real number (&theta; = 0&deg; or 180&deg;).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q23: State the condition for two non-zero vectors A and B to be perpendicular (orthogonal).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      Two vectors are orthogonal if their scalar (dot) product is identically zero: <b>A &bull; B = 0 &rArr; A B cos 90&deg; = 0</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q24: State the condition for two non-zero vectors A and B to be parallel.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      Two vectors are parallel if their vector (cross) product is a null vector: <b>A &times; B = 0 &rArr; A B sin 0&deg; = 0</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q25: Write the scalar (dot) product formula of two vectors A and B.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The scalar product is: <b>A &bull; B = A B cos &theta;</b>, where &theta; is the angle between A and B (0 le \theta le pi).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q26: Write the vector (cross) product formula of two vectors A and B.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The vector product is: <b>A &times; B = (A B sin &theta;) n̂</b>, where n̂ is a unit vector perpendicular to the plane containing A and B given by the right-hand rule.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q27: State the values of î • î, ĵ • ĵ, and k̂ • k̂.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      Since the angle between identical unit vectors is 0&deg; (cos 0&deg; = 1): <b>î &bull; î = ĵ &bull; ĵ = k̂ &bull; k̂ = 1</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q28: State the values of î • ĵ, ĵ • k̂, and k̂ • î.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      Since orthogonal axes meet at 90&deg; (cos 90&deg; = 0): <b>î &bull; ĵ = ĵ &bull; k̂ = k̂ &bull; î = 0</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q29: State the values of î × ĵ, ĵ × k̂, and k̂ × î.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      By the right-hand cyclic rule: <b>î &times; ĵ = k̂</b>, <b>ĵ &times; k̂ = î</b>, and <b>k̂ &times; î = ĵ</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q30: What is the cross product of any vector with itself (A × A)?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      Since the angle between a vector and itself is 0&deg; (sin 0&deg; = 0): <b>A &times; A = 0 (Null vector)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q31: Define Projectile Motion.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      Projectile motion is the two-dimensional parabolic motion of an object projected into space with an initial velocity, moving under the constant downward acceleration of gravity (g) in the absence of air resistance.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q32: What is the shape of the trajectory of a projectile fired in a gravitational field?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The path or trajectory of a projectile is a <b>Parabola</b> (represented mathematically by the quadratic equation y = Ax - Bx<sup>2</sup>).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q33: What is the vertical component of velocity of a projectile at its highest peak?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      At the maximum height point, the vertical component of velocity is momentarily zero: <b>v<sub>y</sub> = 0</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q34: What is the magnitude of velocity of a projectile at its highest point if fired with speed u at angle θ?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      At the highest point, only the horizontal velocity component survives: <b>v = v<sub>x</sub> = u cos &theta;</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q35: What is the angle between the velocity vector and acceleration vector of a projectile at its highest point?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      At the highest point, velocity is purely horizontal and acceleration due to gravity is purely vertical downward, making the angle <b>90&deg; (perpendicular)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q36: At what angle of projection is the horizontal range of a projectile maximum?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The horizontal range is maximum when sin 2&theta; = 1 &rArr; 2&theta; = 90&deg; &rArr; <b>&theta; = 45&deg;</b>, with value <b>R<sub>max</sub> = u<sup>2</sup> / g</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q37: What is the relation between two angles of projection that give the same horizontal range for a given launch speed?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      The ranges are identical for <b>complementary angles</b>: <b>&theta;</b> and <b>(90&deg; - &theta;)</b> (e.g., 30&deg; and 60&deg; give identical range).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q38: Define Uniform Circular Motion (UCM).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      Uniform Circular Motion is the motion of a particle along a circular perimeter of radius r at a constant linear speed v (or constant angular velocity &omega;).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q39: Write the formula for centripetal acceleration in terms of linear velocity and radius.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      Centripetal acceleration: <b>a<sub>c</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span></span> = &omega;<sup>2</sup> r</b>, directed radially inward toward the center of the circular path.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q40: How much work is done by the centripetal force on a particle executing uniform circular motion?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Physical Reasoning:</b>\n      <b>Zero Joules (W = 0).</b> Centripetal force acts radially inward, which is always perpendicular to the instantaneous tangential displacement (cos 90&deg; = 0 &rArr; dW = F &bull; ds = 0).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION B: SA QUESTIONS -->\n  <div style=\"margin-top: 35px; margin-bottom: 15px; border-bottom: 2px solid #8E2DE2; padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold;\">\n      Section B: Short Answer (SA) Questions (2 & 3 Marks each) &bull; Q41 to Q80\n    </h2>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; margin: 4px 0 0 0; font-size: 14px;\">\n      Numerical problem-solving, vector resolution and dot/cross product calculations, relative velocity, and projectile parameters.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q41: A particle moves along the x-axis such that its position is given by x(t) = 3 t³ - 6 t² + 4 t + 2 (where x is in meters and t in seconds). Find: (a) Instantaneous velocity at t = 2 s, (b) Instantaneous acceleration at t = 2 s, (c) Instant when acceleration vanishes.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      <b>(a) Velocity:</b> v(t) = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dx</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span>(3t<sup>3</sup> - 6t<sup>2</sup> + 4t + 2) = 9t<sup>2</sup> - 12t + 4.<br>At t = 2 s: v(2) = 9(2)<sup>2</sup> - 12(2) + 4 = 36 - 24 + 4 = <b>16 m s<sup>-1</sup></b>.<br><b>(b) Acceleration:</b> a(t) = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dv</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> = 18t - 12.<br>At t = 2 s: a(2) = 18(2) - 12 = 36 - 12 = <b>24 m s<sup>-2</sup></b>.<br><b>(c) Zero Acceleration Instant:</b> a(t) = 0 &rArr; 18t - 12 = 0 &rArr; t = 12/18 = <b>0.67 s (2/3 s)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q42: A car traveling at 72 km/h is brought to rest over a distance of 40 m by applying uniform brakes. Calculate: (a) Retardation of the car, (b) Time taken to come to complete stop.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Initial velocity u = 72 km/h = 72 &times; (5/18) = <b>20 m s<sup>-1</sup></b>. Final velocity v = 0. Stopping distance s = 40 m.<br><b>(a) Retardation:</b> Using v<sup>2</sup> = u<sup>2</sup> + 2as:<br>0 = (20)<sup>2</sup> + 2a(40) &rArr; 80a = -400 &rArr; a = -5 m s<sup>-2</sup>.<br><b>Retardation = 5 m s<sup>-2</sup></b>.<br><b>(b) Time taken:</b> Using v = u + at &rArr; 0 = 20 - 5t &rArr; <b>t = 4 seconds</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q43: A ball is thrown vertically upwards with a speed of 19.6 m/s from the top of a tower 39.2 m high. Calculate: (a) Maximum height reached above the ground, (b) Total time taken to hit the ground (g = 9.8 m/s²).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      <b>(a) Maximum height above tower:</b> At peak, v = 0. v<sup>2</sup> = u<sup>2</sup> - 2gh<sub>1</sub> &rArr; 0 = (19.6)<sup>2</sup> - 2(9.8)h<sub>1</sub> &rArr; h<sub>1</sub> = (19.6)<sup>2</sup> / 19.6 = <b>19.6 m</b>.<br>Height above ground = 39.2 + 19.6 = <b>58.8 m</b>.<br><b>(b) Total time of flight:</b> Choosing upward direction as positive, net displacement y = -39.2 m, u = +19.6 m/s, a = -9.8 m/s<sup>2</sup>.<br>y = ut - &frac12;gt<sup>2</sup> &rArr; -39.2 = 19.6t - 4.9t<sup>2</sup>.<br>Divide by 4.9: -8 = 4t - t<sup>2</sup> &rArr; t<sup>2</sup> - 4t - 8 = 0.<br>Quadratic formula: t = [4 &plusmn; &radic;(16 - 4(1)(-8))] / 2 = [4 &plusmn; &radic;48] / 2 = 2 + 3.464 = <b>5.46 seconds</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q44: Explain with diagrams what is meant by Stopping Distance and Reaction Time of a driver. Derive the expression for stopping distance.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      <b>Reaction Time (t_r):</b> The elapsed time between a driver observing a hazard and physically actuating the brake pedal. During this time, the car continues forward with constant speed u, covering reaction distance: d<sub>r</sub> = u &times; t<sub>r</sub>.<br><b>Stopping Distance (d_s):</b> The distance traveled after the brakes are applied until the vehicle stops. Using v<sup>2</sup> = u<sup>2</sup> - 2as where v = 0:<br>0 = u<sup>2</sup> - 2ad<sub>s</sub> &rArr; <b>d<sub>s</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 a</span></span></b>.<br>Total stopping distance = d<sub>r</sub> + d<sub>s</sub> = <b>u t<sub>r</sub> + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 a</span></span></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q45: Two trains 120 m and 80 m long are running in opposite directions with velocities 42 km/h and 30 km/h. In what time will they completely cross each other?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Total distance to be covered for complete crossing = L<sub>1</sub> + L<sub>2</sub> = 120 m + 80 m = <b>200 m</b>.<br>Since running in opposite directions, relative speed = v<sub>1</sub> + v<sub>2</sub>.<br>v<sub>rel</sub> = 42 + 30 = 72 km/h = 72 &times; (5/18) = <b>20 m s<sup>-1</sup></b>.<br>Time to cross = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">Total Distance</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v<sub>rel</sub></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">200 m</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">20 m s<sup>-1</sup></span></span> = <b>10 seconds</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q46: Derive the relation for relative velocity of body A with respect to body B (v_AB = v_A - v_B) in one dimension.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Let positions of A and B at time t be x<sub>A</sub>(t) and x<sub>B</sub>(t).<br>Position of A relative to B is: x<sub>AB</sub>(t) = x<sub>A</sub>(t) - x<sub>B</sub>(t).<br>Differentiating with respect to time t:<br><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d x<sub>AB</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d x<sub>A</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d x<sub>B</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span>.<br>By definition of instantaneous velocity:<br><b>v<sub>AB</sub> = v<sub>A</sub> - v<sub>B</sub></b>.<br>Similarly, velocity of B relative to A is <b>v<sub>BA</sub> = v<sub>B</sub> - v<sub>A</sub> = -v<sub>AB</sub></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q47: If |A + B| = |A - B|, prove analytically that the angle between vectors A and B is 90°.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Given: |A + B| = |A - B|.<br>Squaring both sides:<br>|A + B|<sup>2</sup> = |A - B|<sup>2</sup>.<br>A<sup>2</sup> + B<sup>2</sup> + 2 A &bull; B = A<sup>2</sup> + B<sup>2</sup> - 2 A &bull; B.<br>Subtract A<sup>2</sup> + B<sup>2</sup> from both sides:<br>2 A &bull; B = -2 A &bull; B &rArr; 4 A &bull; B = 0 &rArr; A &bull; B = 0.<br>A B cos &theta; = 0. Since A &ne; 0 and B &ne; 0: cos &theta; = 0 &rArr; <b>&theta; = 90&deg;</b>.<br>Hence vectors A and B are strictly mutually perpendicular.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q48: Find the unit vector in the direction of vector A = 3 î - 4 ĵ + 12 k̂. Also find its direction cosines.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Magnitude of A: |A| = &radic;[3<sup>2</sup> + (-4)<sup>2</sup> + 12<sup>2</sup>] = &radic;[9 + 16 + 144] = &radic;169 = <b>13</b>.<br><b>Unit Vector:</b> Â = A / |A| = <b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">3</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">13</span></span> î - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">13</span></span> ĵ + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">12</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">13</span></span> k̂</b>.<br><b>Direction Cosines:</b><br>l = cos &alpha; = <b>3/13</b>, m = cos &beta; = <b>-4/13</b>, n = cos &gamma; = <b>12/13</b>.<br>(Verification: l<sup>2</sup> + m<sup>2</sup> + n<sup>2</sup> = 9/169 + 16/169 + 144/169 = 169/169 = 1).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q49: Find the angle between the two vectors A = 2 î + 2 ĵ - k̂ and B = 6 î - 3 ĵ + 2 k̂.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Dot product: A &bull; B = (2)(6) + (2)(-3) + (-1)(2) = 12 - 6 - 2 = <b>4</b>.<br>Magnitude of A: |A| = &radic;[2<sup>2</sup> + 2<sup>2</sup> + (-1)<sup>2</sup>] = &radic;[4 + 4 + 1] = &radic;9 = <b>3</b>.<br>Magnitude of B: |B| = &radic;[6<sup>2</sup> + (-3)<sup>2</sup> + 2<sup>2</sup>] = &radic;[36 + 9 + 4] = &radic;49 = <b>7</b>.<br>cos &theta; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A &bull; B</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">|A| |B|</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">3 &times; 7</span></span> = <b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">21</span></span></b>.<br><b>&theta; = cos<sup>-1</sup>(4 / 21) &approx; 79.02&deg;</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q50: Find the scalar and vector projections of vector A = î + 3 ĵ + 7 k̂ on vector B = 7 î - ĵ + 8 k̂.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      <b>1. Dot product:</b> A &bull; B = (1)(7) + (3)(-1) + (7)(8) = 7 - 3 + 56 = <b>60</b>.<br><b>2. Magnitude of B:</b> |B| = &radic;[7<sup>2</sup> + (-1)<sup>2</sup> + 8<sup>2</sup>] = &radic;[49 + 1 + 64] = &radic;114.<br><b>Scalar Projection of A on B:</b> A &bull; B̂ = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A &bull; B</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">|B|</span></span> = <b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">60</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;114</span></span></b>.<br><b>Vector Projection of A on B:</b> (A &bull; B̂) B̂ = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">60</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">114</span></span> (7 î - ĵ + 8 k̂) = <b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">10</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">19</span></span> (7 î - ĵ + 8 k̂)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q51: Determine a unit vector perpendicular to both vectors A = 2 î + ĵ + k̂ and B = î - ĵ + 2 k̂.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      A vector perpendicular to both A and B is given by their cross product A &times; B:<br>A &times; B = | î &nbsp; ĵ &nbsp; k̂ |<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;| 2 &nbsp; 1 &nbsp; 1 |<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;| 1 &nbsp;-1 &nbsp; 2 |<br>= î[(1)(2) - (1)(-1)] - ĵ[(2)(2) - (1)(1)] + k̂[(2)(-1) - (1)(1)]<br>= î(2 + 1) - ĵ(4 - 1) + k̂(-2 - 1) = <b>3 î - 3 ĵ - 3 k̂</b>.<br>Magnitude: |A &times; B| = &radic;[3<sup>2</sup> + (-3)<sup>2</sup> + (-3)<sup>2</sup>] = &radic;[9 + 9 + 9] = &radic;27 = 3&radic;3.<br>Unit vector n̂ = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A &times; B</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">|A &times; B|</span></span> = <b>&plusmn; <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;3</span></span> (î - ĵ - k̂)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q52: Calculate the area of a parallelogram whose adjacent sides are given by vectors A = 3 î + ĵ + 4 k̂ and B = î - ĵ + k̂.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Area of parallelogram = |A &times; B|.<br>A &times; B = | î &nbsp; ĵ &nbsp; k̂ |<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;| 3 &nbsp; 1 &nbsp; 4 |<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;| 1 &nbsp;-1 &nbsp; 1 |<br>= î(1 - (-4)) - ĵ(3 - 4) + k̂(-3 - 1) = <b>5 î + ĵ - 4 k̂</b>.<br>Area = &radic;[5<sup>2</sup> + 1<sup>2</sup> + (-4)<sup>2</sup>] = &radic;[25 + 1 + 16] = <b>&radic;42 &approx; 6.48 sq. units</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q53: Prove that the area of a triangle formed by two vectors A and B as adjacent sides is ½ |A × B|.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Let vectors A and B act at angle &theta;. Base of triangle = |A|. Height of triangle = |B| sin &theta;.<br>Area of triangle = &frac12; &times; Base &times; Height = &frac12; |A| |B| sin &theta;.<br>Since |A &times; B| = |A| |B| sin &theta;, it follows directly that:<br><b>Area of Triangle = &frac12; |A &times; B|</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q54: Rain is falling vertically downwards with speed 30 m/s. A cyclist rides horizontally with speed 10 m/s from North to South. In what direction should the cyclist hold an umbrella to protect against rain?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Velocity of rain: v<sub>R</sub> = -30 ĵ. Velocity of cyclist: v<sub>C</sub> = -10 î (taking South as positive X).<br>Relative velocity of rain with respect to cyclist: v<sub>RC</sub> = v<sub>R</sub> - v<sub>C</sub> = -30 ĵ - (-10 î) = 10 î - 30 ĵ.<br>Magnitude: |v<sub>RC</sub>| = &radic;[10<sup>2</sup> + (-30)<sup>2</sup>] = &radic;1000 &approx; 31.62 m/s.<br>Direction: tan &theta; = |v<sub>C</sub>| / |v<sub>R</sub>| = 10 / 30 = 1/3.<br>&theta; = tan<sup>-1</sup>(1/3) &approx; <b>18.43&deg; with the vertical towards South</b>.<br>The cyclist should hold the umbrella tilted at <b>18.4&deg; towards the South</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q55: A boat can row with speed 10 km/h in still water. River flows at 5 km/h. In what direction should the boat head to cross the river along the shortest path? What will be the resultant crossing speed?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      For the shortest path (straight across, perpendicular to river flow), resultant velocity must be directed straight across.<br>Let angle with river upstream be &theta;.<br>sin &alpha; = v<sub>river</sub> / v<sub>boat</sub> = 5 / 10 = 0.5 &rArr; &alpha; = 30&deg;.<br>Therefore, the boat must head at an angle of <b>90&deg; + 30&deg; = 120&deg; upstream</b> relative to river flow direction.<br>Resultant crossing speed: v<sub>res</sub> = &radic;[v<sub>boat</sub><sup>2</sup> - v<sub>river</sub><sup>2</sup>] = &radic;[10<sup>2</sup> - 5<sup>2</sup>] = &radic;75 = <b>5&radic;3 km/h &approx; 8.66 km/h</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q56: A projectile is fired with an initial speed of 98 m/s at an angle of 30° with the horizontal. Calculate: (a) Time of flight, (b) Maximum height attained, (c) Horizontal range (g = 9.8 m/s²).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      u = 98 m/s, &theta; = 30&deg;, g = 9.8 m/s<sup>2</sup>. sin 30&deg; = 0.5, cos 30&deg; = &radic;3/2, sin 60&deg; = &radic;3/2.<br><b>(a) Time of flight:</b> T = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 u sin &theta;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 &times; 98 &times; 0.5</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">9.8</span></span> = <b>10 seconds</b>.<br><b>(b) Maximum height:</b> H = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> sin<sup>2</sup> &theta;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2g</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">(98)<sup>2</sup> &times; (0.5)<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 &times; 9.8</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">9604 &times; 0.25</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">19.6</span></span> = <b>122.5 meters</b>.<br><b>(c) Horizontal range:</b> R = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> sin 2&theta;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">(98)<sup>2</sup> &times; sin 60&deg;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">9.8</span></span> = 980 &times; <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">&radic;3</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> = <b>490&radic;3 &approx; 848.7 meters</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q57: Prove analytically that the horizontal range of a projectile is the same for two angles of projection θ and (90° - θ).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Range formula: R(&theta;) = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> sin 2&theta;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>.<br>For angle (90&deg; - &theta;):<br>R(90&deg; - &theta;) = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> sin[2(90&deg; - &theta;)]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> sin(180&deg; - 2&theta;)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>.<br>Since trigonometric identity gives sin(180&deg; - A) = sin A:<br>sin(180&deg; - 2&theta;) = sin 2&theta;.<br>Therefore: <b>R(90&deg; - &theta;) = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> sin 2&theta;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span> = R(&theta;)</b>.<br>Thus, for a given initial velocity, complementary angles yield identical horizontal ranges.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q58: Prove that the maximum height H and horizontal range R of a projectile are related by: R = 4 H cot θ. What does this become at maximum range?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      H = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> sin<sup>2</sup> &theta;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2g</span></span> &rArr; u<sup>2</sup> / g = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2H</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">sin<sup>2</sup> &theta;</span></span>.<br>Substitute into Range R = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> (2 sin &theta; cos &theta;)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>:<br>R = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2H</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">sin<sup>2</sup> &theta;</span></span>\right) (2 sin &theta; cos &theta;) = 4H <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">cos &theta;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">sin &theta;</span></span> = <b>4 H cot &theta;</b>.<br>At maximum range, &theta; = 45&deg; (cot 45&deg; = 1):<br><b>R<sub>max</sub> = 4 H</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q59: A projectile has an initial kinetic energy K₀. Find its kinetic energy at the highest point of its trajectory.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Initial kinetic energy: K<sub>0</sub> = &frac12; m u<sup>2</sup>.<br>At the highest point, vertical velocity v<sub>y</sub> = 0. Only horizontal component v<sub>x</sub> = u cos &theta; remains.<br>Kinetic energy at peak: K<sub>top</sub> = &frac12; m v<sub>x</sub><sup>2</sup> = &frac12; m (u cos &theta;)<sup>2</sup> = (&frac12; m u<sup>2</sup>) cos<sup>2</sup> &theta;.<br><b>K<sub>top</sub> = K<sub>0</sub> cos<sup>2</sup> &theta;</b>.<br>For example, if projected at 60&deg;, K<sub>top</sub> = K<sub>0</sub> cos<sup>2</sup> 60&deg; = K<sub>0</sub> (1/2)<sup>2</sup> = K<sub>0</sub> / 4.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q60: Calculate the angular speed (ω) of: (a) Second hand, (b) Minute hand, (c) Hour hand of a clock in rad/s.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Angular speed &omega; = 2&pi; / T:<br><b>(a) Second hand:</b> T = 60 s &rArr; &omega; = 2&pi; / 60 = <b>&pi; / 30 rad s<sup>-1</sup> &approx; 0.1047 rad s<sup>-1</sup></b>.<br><b>(b) Minute hand:</b> T = 60 min = 3600 s &rArr; &omega; = 2&pi; / 3600 = <b>&pi; / 1800 rad s<sup>-1</sup> &approx; 1.745 &times; 10<sup>-3</sup> rad s<sup>-1</sup></b>.<br><b>(c) Hour hand:</b> T = 12 h = 12 &times; 3600 = 43200 s &rArr; &omega; = 2&pi; / 43200 = <b>&pi; / 21600 rad s<sup>-1</sup> &approx; 1.454 &times; 10<sup>-4</sup> rad s<sup>-1</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q61: A particle is moving on a circular track of radius 20 m with a speed that increases uniformly at the rate of 3 m/s². At the instant when speed is 20 m/s, find: (a) Centripetal acceleration, (b) Total acceleration magnitude.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      r = 20 m, tangential acceleration a<sub>t</sub> = dv/dt = 3 m s<sup>-2</sup>, instantaneous speed v = 20 m/s.<br><b>(a) Centripetal acceleration:</b> a<sub>c</sub> = v<sup>2</sup> / r = (20)<sup>2</sup> / 20 = <b>20 m s<sup>-2</sup></b> (radially inward).<br><b>(b) Total acceleration:</b> Since a<sub>t</sub> and a<sub>c</sub> are perpendicular:<br>a = &radic;[a<sub>t</sub><sup>2</sup> + a<sub>c</sub><sup>2</sup>] = &radic;[3<sup>2</sup> + 20<sup>2</sup>] = &radic;[9 + 400] = &radic;409 = <b>20.22 m s<sup>-2</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q62: Explain why a stone tied to a string and whirled in a horizontal circle flies off tangentially when the string breaks.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      By Newton's First Law of Motion (Inertia of Direction), an object moving along a curved path possesses an instantaneous tangential velocity at every point. The string provides the necessary inward centripetal tension to bend the path. When the string breaks, tension instantly drops to zero, and the stone continues in a straight line along the instantaneous <b>tangent</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q63: A ball is dropped from a balloon ascending vertically at 10 m/s when the balloon is at a height of 75 m. How long does the ball take to reach the ground? (g = 10 m/s²)</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Initial velocity of ball = balloon velocity: u = +10 m/s. Height y = -75 m, a = -g = -10 m/s<sup>2</sup>.<br>y = u t - &frac12; g t<sup>2</sup> &rArr; -75 = 10t - 5t<sup>2</sup>.<br>Divide by 5: -15 = 2t - t<sup>2</sup> &rArr; t<sup>2</sup> - 2t - 15 = 0.<br>Factorize: (t - 5)(t + 3) = 0.<br>Since time cannot be negative: <b>t = 5 seconds</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q64: Derive the relation between linear acceleration (a) and angular acceleration (α) in circular motion.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Linear velocity relates to angular velocity by v = r &omega;.<br>Differentiating with respect to time t:<br><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dv</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span>(r &omega;) = r <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d&omega;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span>.<br>Since tangential acceleration is a<sub>t</sub> = dv/dt and angular acceleration is &alpha; = d&omega;/dt:<br><b>a<sub>t</sub> = r &alpha; &nbsp;&bull;&nbsp; a = &alpha; &times; r</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q65: Two bodies are thrown with the same initial velocity at angles θ₁ and θ₂ with the horizontal. If their maximum heights are H₁ and H₂, prove that (H₁ / H₂) = tan² θ₁ when θ₁ + θ₂ = 90°.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      H<sub>1</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> sin<sup>2</sup> &theta;<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2g</span></span> and H<sub>2</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> sin<sup>2</sup> &theta;<sub>2</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2g</span></span>.<br>Since &theta;<sub>1</sub> + &theta;<sub>2</sub> = 90&deg; &rArr; &theta;<sub>2</sub> = 90&deg; - &theta;<sub>1</sub>:<br>sin &theta;<sub>2</sub> = sin(90&deg; - &theta;<sub>1</sub>) = cos &theta;<sub>1</sub>.<br>Therefore: H<sub>2</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> cos<sup>2</sup> &theta;<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2g</span></span>.<br>Ratio: <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">H<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">H<sub>2</sub></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">sin<sup>2</sup> &theta;<sub>1</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">cos<sup>2</sup> &theta;<sub>1</sub></span></span> = <b>tan<sup>2</sup> &theta;<sub>1</sub></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q66: State and prove the Triangle Law of Vector Addition geometrically.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      <b>Statement:</b> If two vectors are represented in magnitude and direction by two sides of a triangle taken in order, their resultant is represented by the third side taken in reverse order.<br><b>Proof:</b> Let vector P = OA and vector Q = AB. Draw BC &perp; OA extended.<br>In &Delta;ABC: AC = Q cos &theta;, BC = Q sin &theta;.<br>In right &Delta;OCB: OB<sup>2</sup> = OC<sup>2</sup> + BC<sup>2</sup> = (OA + AC)<sup>2</sup> + BC<sup>2</sup><br>R<sup>2</sup> = (P + Q cos &theta;)<sup>2</sup> + (Q sin &theta;)<sup>2</sup> = P<sup>2</sup> + Q<sup>2</sup> cos<sup>2</sup> &theta; + 2PQ cos &theta; + Q<sup>2</sup> sin<sup>2</sup> &theta;<br>R<sup>2</sup> = P<sup>2</sup> + Q<sup>2</sup>(cos<sup>2</sup> &theta; + sin<sup>2</sup> &theta;) + 2PQ cos &theta; = P<sup>2</sup> + Q<sup>2</sup> + 2PQ cos &theta;.<br><b>R = &radic;[P<sup>2</sup> + Q<sup>2</sup> + 2PQ cos &theta;]</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q67: If A = 2 î + 3 ĵ + 4 k̂ and B = 4 î + 3 ĵ + 2 k̂, find: (a) A • B, (b) Magnitude of A × B.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      <b>(a) A &bull; B:</b> (2)(4) + (3)(3) + (4)(2) = 8 + 9 + 8 = <b>25</b>.<br><b>(b) A &times; B:</b><br>| î &nbsp; ĵ &nbsp; k̂ |<br>| 2 &nbsp; 3 &nbsp; 4 |<br>| 4 &nbsp; 3 &nbsp; 2 |<br>= î(6 - 12) - ĵ(4 - 16) + k̂(6 - 12) = -6 î + 12 ĵ - 6 k̂.<br>Magnitude |A &times; B| = &radic;[(-6)<sup>2</sup> + 12<sup>2</sup> + (-6)<sup>2</sup>] = &radic;[36 + 144 + 36] = &radic;216 = <b>6&radic;6 &approx; 14.70</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q68: Prove that vector cross product is anti-commutative: A × B = - (B × A).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      By definition: A &times; B = (A B sin &theta;) n̂<sub>1</sub>, where n̂<sub>1</sub> points in direction determined by right-hand thumb rule curling from A to B.<br>Similarly: B &times; A = (B A sin &theta;) n̂<sub>2</sub>, where n̂<sub>2</sub> points curling from B to A.<br>Since turning from B to A is opposite to turning from A to B, n̂<sub>2</sub> = -n̂<sub>1</sub>.<br>Therefore: B &times; A = - (A B sin &theta;) n̂<sub>1</sub> = - (A &times; B) &rArr; <b>A &times; B = - (B &times; A)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q69: Show that for two vectors to be parallel, their corresponding components must be in constant proportion.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Two vectors A and B are parallel if A &times; B = 0.<br>A &times; B = (A<sub>y</sub> B<sub>z</sub> - A<sub>z</sub> B<sub>y</sub>) î + (A<sub>z</sub> B<sub>x</sub> - A<sub>x</sub> B<sub>z</sub>) ĵ + (A<sub>x</sub> B<sub>y</sub> - A<sub>y</sub> B<sub>x</sub>) k̂ = 0.<br>Equating each component to zero:<br>A<sub>y</sub>/B<sub>y</sub> = A<sub>z</sub>/B<sub>z</sub>, A<sub>z</sub>/B<sub>z</sub> = A<sub>x</sub>/B<sub>x</sub>, A<sub>x</sub>/B<sub>x</sub> = A<sub>y</sub>/B<sub>y</sub>.<br>Therefore: <b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A<sub>x</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">B<sub>x</sub></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A<sub>y</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">B<sub>y</sub></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A<sub>z</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">B<sub>z</sub></span></span> = \text{Constant}</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q70: A body starts from rest with acceleration a₁ for time t₁ and then decelerates to rest at rate a₂ in time t₂. If total distance is s, prove that s = ½ a₁ a₂ T² / (a₁ + a₂), where T = t₁ + t₂.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Maximum velocity attained: v<sub>max</sub> = a<sub>1</sub> t<sub>1</sub> = a<sub>2</sub> t<sub>2</sub>.<br>t<sub>1</sub> = v<sub>max</sub> / a<sub>1</sub> and t<sub>2</sub> = v<sub>max</sub> / a<sub>2</sub>.<br>Total time T = t<sub>1</sub> + t<sub>2</sub> = v<sub>max</sub> (1/a<sub>1</sub> + 1/a<sub>2</sub>) = v<sub>max</sub> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a<sub>1</sub> + a<sub>2</sub></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a<sub>1</sub> a<sub>2</sub></span></span> &rArr; v<sub>max</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a<sub>1</sub> a<sub>2</sub> T</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a<sub>1</sub> + a<sub>2</sub></span></span>.<br>Total distance s = Area of v-t triangle = &frac12; &times; Base &times; Height = &frac12; T v<sub>max</sub>.<br>s = &frac12; T left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a<sub>1</sub> a<sub>2</sub> T</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a<sub>1</sub> + a<sub>2</sub></span></span>\right) = <b><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">1</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a<sub>1</sub> a<sub>2</sub> T<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a<sub>1</sub> + a<sub>2</sub></span></span></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q71: A bomb is released from an airplane flying horizontally with speed u at altitude H. Find: (a) Time taken to strike the ground, (b) Horizontal distance traveled, (c) Speed when striking ground.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Horizontal velocity u<sub>x</sub> = u, initial vertical velocity u<sub>y</sub> = 0.<br><b>(a) Time:</b> Vertical motion: H = &frac12; g t<sup>2</sup> &rArr; <b>t = &radic;(2H / g)</b>.<br><b>(b) Horizontal distance:</b> x = u<sub>x</sub> t = <b>u &radic;(2H / g)</b>.<br><b>(c) Striking velocity:</b> v<sub>x</sub> = u, v<sub>y</sub> = &radic;(2gH).<br>Resultant speed: <b>v = &radic;[v<sub>x</sub><sup>2</sup> + v<sub>y</sub><sup>2</sup>] = &radic;(u<sup>2</sup> + 2gH)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q72: Find the angle of projection for which the horizontal range of a projectile is equal to its maximum height.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Given: R = H.<br><span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> (2 sin &theta; cos &theta;)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> sin<sup>2</sup> &theta;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2g</span></span>.<br>Cancel u<sup>2</sup> / g and sin &theta;: 2 cos &theta; = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">sin &theta;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span> &rArr; sin &theta; / cos &theta; = 4 &rArr; <b>tan &theta; = 4</b>.<br><b>&theta; = tan<sup>-1</sup>(4) &approx; 75.96&deg;</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q73: If R is the horizontal range for projection angle θ and h₁, h₂ are the maximum heights for complementary angles θ and (90° - θ), prove that R = 4 √(h₁ h₂).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      h<sub>1</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> sin<sup>2</sup> &theta;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2g</span></span> and h<sub>2</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> cos<sup>2</sup> &theta;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2g</span></span>.<br>Multiply h<sub>1</sub> and h<sub>2</sub>:<br>h<sub>1</sub> h<sub>2</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>4</sup> sin<sup>2</sup> &theta; cos<sup>2</sup> &theta;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 g<sup>2</sup></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>4</sup> (2 sin &theta; cos &theta;)<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">16 g<sup>2</sup></span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>4</sup> sin<sup>2</sup> 2&theta;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">16 g<sup>2</sup></span></span>.<br>Taking square root: &radic;(h<sub>1</sub> h<sub>2</sub>) = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> sin 2&theta;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4 g</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4</span></span>.<br>Therefore: <b>R = 4 &radic;(h<sub>1</sub> h<sub>2</sub>)</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q74: A projectile passes through two points at the same height h above the ground at times t₁ and t₂. Prove that: (a) t₁ + t₂ = T (time of flight), (b) h = ½ g t₁ t₂.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Vertical equation: h = (u sin &theta;) t - &frac12; g t<sup>2</sup> &rArr; &frac12; g t<sup>2</sup> - (u sin &theta;) t + h = 0 &rArr; t<sup>2</sup> - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 u sin &theta;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span> t + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2h</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span> = 0.<br>The roots of this quadratic equation are the two passage times t<sub>1</sub> and t<sub>2</sub>.<br><b>(a) Sum of roots:</b> t<sub>1</sub> + t<sub>2</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 u sin &theta;</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span> = <b>T (Total time of flight)</b>.<br><b>(b) Product of roots:</b> t<sub>1</sub> t<sub>2</sub> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2h</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span> &rArr; <b>h = &frac12; g t<sub>1</sub> t<sub>2</sub></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q75: Find the work done by force F = (2 î - 3 ĵ + 4 k̂) N in displacing a body from point A(1, 2, 3) m to point B(4, 5, 6) m.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Displacement vector: &Delta;r = r<sub>B</sub> - r<sub>A</sub> = (4 - 1) î + (5 - 2) ĵ + (6 - 3) k̂ = <b>3 î + 3 ĵ + 3 k̂ m</b>.<br>Work done W = F &bull; &Delta;r = (2)(3) + (-3)(3) + (4)(3) = 6 - 9 + 12 = <b>9 Joules</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q76: Find the torque vector τ = r × F about the origin for position vector r = (3 î + 2 ĵ + k̂) m and force F = (5 î + 2 ĵ - 7 k̂) N.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      &tau; = r &times; F = | î &nbsp; ĵ &nbsp; k̂ |<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;| 3 &nbsp; 2 &nbsp; 1 |<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;| 5 &nbsp; 2 &nbsp;-7 |<br>= î[(2)(-7) - (1)(2)] - ĵ[(3)(-7) - (1)(5)] + k̂[(3)(2) - (2)(5)]<br>= î(-14 - 2) - ĵ(-21 - 5) + k̂(6 - 10) = <b>-16 î + 26 ĵ - 4 k̂ N m</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q77: A swimmer wishes to cross a 500 m wide river flowing at 3 km/h. If his swimming speed in still water is 5 km/h, calculate: (a) Shortest time to cross, (b) Resulting drift downstream.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Width d = 500 m = 0.5 km, v<sub>s</sub> = 5 km/h, v<sub>r</sub> = 3 km/h.<br><b>(a) Shortest time:</b> To cross in minimum time, swimmer must steer strictly perpendicular to flow (along width).<br>t<sub>min</sub> = d / v<sub>s</sub> = 0.5 / 5 = 0.1 hour = <b>6 minutes (360 seconds)</b>.<br><b>(b) Drift downstream:</b> Drift x = v<sub>r</sub> &times; t<sub>min</sub> = 3 km/h &times; 0.1 h = 0.3 km = <b>300 meters</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q78: Explain why an object moving in a circle with constant speed has non-zero acceleration, while along a straight line constant speed implies zero acceleration.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      Acceleration is the time rate of change of the velocity vector: a = dv/dt. Velocity has two components: magnitude (speed) and direction.<br>1. In rectilinear motion, direction is fixed. If speed is constant, dv/dt = 0 (zero acceleration).<br>2. In circular motion, although speed is constant, the spatial direction of velocity changes continuously at every instant. This continuous turning of the velocity vector requires an acceleration directed toward the center: <b>centripetal acceleration a<sub>c</sub> = v<sup>2</sup>/r &ne; 0</b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q79: State the Polygon Law of Vector Addition. How is it applied to determine equilibrium of concurrent forces?</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      <b>Polygon Law Statement:</b> If a number of vectors are represented in magnitude and direction by the sides of an open polygon taken in the same cyclic order, then their resultant vector is represented in magnitude and direction by the closing side of the polygon taken in the reverse direction.<br><b>Equilibrium Application:</b> If a system of concurrent coplanar forces forms a <b>closed polygon</b> taken in the same cyclic order, the closing side is zero, meaning the net resultant force is identically zero (&Sigma; F = 0), establishing translational equilibrium.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q80: A particle executes uniform circular motion with frequency 120 rpm on a track of radius 25 cm. Calculate: (a) Angular speed, (b) Linear speed, (c) Centripetal acceleration.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Solution & Step-by-Step Mathematical Proof:</b>\n      r = 25 cm = 0.25 m, &nu; = 120 rpm = 120 / 60 = <b>2 rev/s (Hz)</b>.<br><b>(a) Angular speed:</b> &omega; = 2&pi; &nu; = 2 &times; 3.1416 &times; 2 = <b>4&pi; rad s<sup>-1</sup> &approx; 12.57 rad s<sup>-1</sup></b>.<br><b>(b) Linear speed:</b> v = r &omega; = 0.25 &times; 4&pi; = &pi; m/s &approx; <b>3.14 m s<sup>-1</sup></b>.<br><b>(c) Centripetal acceleration:</b> a<sub>c</sub> = &omega;<sup>2</sup> r = (4&pi;)<sup>2</sup> &times; 0.25 = 16&pi;<sup>2</sup> &times; 0.25 = 4&pi;<sup>2</sup> &approx; <b>39.48 m s<sup>-2</sup></b>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <!-- SECTION C: LA QUESTIONS -->\n  <div style=\"margin-top: 35px; margin-bottom: 15px; border-bottom: 2px solid #8E2DE2; padding-bottom: 8px;\">\n    <h2 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold;\">\n      Section C: Long Answer (LA) Questions (5 Marks each) &bull; Q81 to Q100\n    </h2>\n    <p style=\"color: #94A3B8; margin: 4px 0 0 0; font-size: 14px;\">\n      Comprehensive multi-step board derivations (graphical & calculus kinematics, projectile parabolic trajectory, vector laws, centripetal acceleration, and master unit synthesis).\n    </p>\n  </div>\n  \n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q81: Derive the three equations of uniformly accelerated rectilinear motion (v = u + at, s = ut + &frac12;at&sup2;, and v&sup2; - u&sup2; = 2as) using the Graphical Method (velocity-time graph).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>Graphical Setup:</b><br>\n      Consider an object moving along a straight line with uniform acceleration a. Let its initial velocity at time t = 0 be u (represented by point A on the velocity axis) and final velocity at time t be v (represented by point B).<br>\n      Plotting the velocity-time (v-t) graph:<br>\n      &bull; OA = CD = u (initial velocity)<br>\n      &bull; OE = CB = v (final velocity)<br>\n      &bull; OC = AD = t (time interval)<br>\n      &bull; BD = BC - CD = v - u (change in velocity).<br><br>\n      <b>1. First Equation of Motion (v = u + at):</b><br>\n      The slope of the v-t graph represents the instantaneous acceleration a:<br>\n      a = \text{Slope of line } AB = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">BD</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">AD</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v - u</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">t</span></span>.<br>\n      Cross-multiplying: v - u = at implies mathbf{v = u + at}.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Second Equation of Motion (s = ut + \frac{1}{2}at<sup>2</sup>):</b><br>\n      The area under the v-t curve between t = 0 and t represents displacement s:<br>\n      s = \text{Area of trapezium } OABC = \text{Area of rectangle } OADC + \text{Area of right triangle } ABD.<br>\n      &bull; \text{Area of rectangle } OADC = OA \times OC = u \times t = ut.<br>\n      &bull; \text{Area of triangle } ABD = \frac{1}{2} \times \text{base } AD \times \text{height } BD = \frac{1}{2} \times t \times (v - u).<br>\n      From the first equation, (v - u) = at. Substituting this gives:<br>\n      \text{Area of triangle } ABD = \frac{1}{2} \times t \times (at) = \frac{1}{2}at<sup>2</sup>.<br>\n      Summing the areas: mathbf{s = ut + \frac{1}{2}at<sup>2</sup>}.<br><br>\n      <b>3. Third Equation of Motion (v<sup>2</sup> - u<sup>2</sup> = 2as):</b><br>\n      Displacement s can also be computed directly using the standard formula for the area of a trapezium:<br>\n      s = \frac{1}{2} \times (\text{sum of parallel sides}) \times (\text{perpendicular distance between them})<br>\n      s = \frac{1}{2}(OA + CB) \times OC = \frac{1}{2}(u + v)t.<br>\n      From the first equation, t = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v - u</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a</span></span>. Substituting t:<br>\n      s = \frac{1}{2}(v + u)left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v - u</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a</span></span>\right) = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v<sup>2</sup> - u<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2a</span></span>.<br>\n      Cross-multiplying yields: mathbf{v<sup>2</sup> - u<sup>2</sup> = 2as} implies mathbf{v<sup>2</sup> = u<sup>2</sup> + 2as}.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q82: Derive the three kinematic equations of uniformly accelerated motion (v = u + at, s = ut + &frac12;at&sup2;, and v&sup2; - u&sup2; = 2as) using the Calculus Method (differentiation and integration).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. First Equation (v = u + at):</b><br>\n      By definition, instantaneous acceleration is a = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dv</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span>.<br>\n      Rearranging: dv = a , dt.<br>\n      Integrating both sides with appropriate boundary conditions: at t = 0, velocity is u; at time t, velocity is v:<br>\n      int_u<sup>v</sup> dv = int<sub>0</sub><sup>t</sup> a , dt.<br>\n      Since acceleration a is constant, it factors out of the integral:<br>\n      [v]_u<sup>v</sup> = a [t]<sub>0</sub><sup>t</sup> implies v - u = a(t - 0) implies mathbf{v = u + at}.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Second Equation (s = ut + \frac{1}{2}at<sup>2</sup>):</b><br>\n      Instantaneous velocity is v = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">ds</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span>, so ds = v , dt.<br>\n      Substitute v = u + at: ds = (u + at)dt.<br>\n      Integrating both sides: at t = 0, displacement s = 0; at time t, displacement is s:<br>\n      int<sub>0</sub><sup>s</sup> ds = int<sub>0</sub><sup>t</sup> (u + at) dt = u int<sub>0</sub><sup>t</sup> dt + a int<sub>0</sub><sup>t</sup> t , dt.<br>\n      [s]<sub>0</sub><sup>s</sup> = u [t]<sub>0</sub><sup>t</sup> + a left[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">t<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>\right]<sub>0</sub><sup>t</sup> implies mathbf{s = ut + \frac{1}{2}at<sup>2</sup>}.<br><br>\n      <b>3. Third Equation (v<sup>2</sup> - u<sup>2</sup> = 2as):</b><br>\n      By the calculus chain rule, acceleration can be expressed as:<br>\n      a = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dv</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dv</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">ds</span></span> cdot <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">ds</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> = v , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dv</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">ds</span></span>.<br>\n      Rearranging terms: a , ds = v , dv.<br>\n      Integrating both sides: when s = 0, velocity is u; when displacement is s, velocity is v:<br>\n      int<sub>0</sub><sup>s</sup> a , ds = int_u<sup>v</sup> v , dv.<br>\n      a [s]<sub>0</sub><sup>s</sup> = left[<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>\right]_u<sup>v</sup> implies a(s - 0) = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v<sup>2</sup> - u<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span></span>.<br>\n      Multiplying by 2: 2as = v<sup>2</sup> - u<sup>2</sup> implies mathbf{v<sup>2</sup> = u<sup>2</sup> + 2as}.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q83: Derive an analytical expression for the distance traversed by a uniformly accelerated body in the n-th second (s_n). Hence, prove Galileo's Law of Odd Numbers for a freely falling body dropped from rest.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Derivation of Distance in n<sup>\text{th</sup>} Second (s_n):</b><br>\n      The distance covered specifically during the n<sup>\text{th</sup>} second is the difference between displacement after n seconds (s_n') and displacement after (n - 1) seconds (s_{n-1}'):<br>\n      s_n = s_n' - s_{n-1}'.<br>\n      Using s = ut + \frac{1}{2}at<sup>2</sup>:<br>\n      &bull; s_n' = u(n) + \frac{1}{2}a(n)<sup>2</sup> = un + \frac{1}{2}an<sup>2</sup>.<br>\n      &bull; s_{n-1}' = u(n - 1) + \frac{1}{2}a(n - 1)<sup>2</sup> = un - u + \frac{1}{2}a(n<sup>2</sup> - 2n + 1) = un - u + \frac{1}{2}an<sup>2</sup> - an + \frac{1}{2}a.<br>\n      Subtracting s_{n-1}' from s_n':<br>\n      s_n = left(un + \frac{1}{2}an<sup>2</sup>\right) - left(un - u + \frac{1}{2}an<sup>2</sup> - an + \frac{1}{2}a\right)<br>\n      s_n = u + an - \frac{1}{2}a = mathbf{u + \frac{a}{2}(2n - 1)}.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Galileo's Law of Odd Numbers:</b><br>\n      Consider a body released from rest (u = 0) under gravity (a = g):<br>\n      s_n = 0 + \frac{g}{2}(2n - 1) = \frac{g}{2}(2n - 1).<br>\n      Calculating distance covered in successive seconds:<br>\n      &bull; For 1<sup>\text{st</sup>} second (n = 1): s_1 = \frac{g}{2}[2(1) - 1] = mathbf{1 left(\frac{g}{2}\right)}.<br>\n      &bull; For 2<sup>\text{nd</sup>} second (n = 2): s_2 = \frac{g}{2}[2(2) - 1] = mathbf{3 left(\frac{g}{2}\right)}.<br>\n      &bull; For 3<sup>\text{rd</sup>} second (n = 3): s_3 = \frac{g}{2}[2(3) - 1] = mathbf{5 left(\frac{g}{2}\right)}.<br>\n      &bull; For 4<sup>\text{th</sup>} second (n = 4): s_4 = \frac{g}{2}[2(4) - 1] = mathbf{7 left(\frac{g}{2}\right)}.<br>\n      Taking the ratio of successive displacements:<br>\n      s_1 : s_2 : s_3 : s_4 : dots = mathbf{1 : 3 : 5 : 7 : dots}<br>\n      This establishes <b>Galileo's Law of Odd Numbers</b>: The distances traversed during equal successive intervals of time by a body falling from rest stand to one another in the ratio of the consecutive odd integers beginning with unity.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q84: State the Parallelogram Law of Vector Addition. Derive the analytical expressions for both the magnitude and direction of the resultant of two vectors inclined at an angle &theta;. Discuss the special cases when &theta; = 0&deg;, 90&deg;, and 180&deg;.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Statement of Parallelogram Law:</b><br>\n      If two vectors acting simultaneously at a point are represented both in magnitude and direction by two adjacent sides of a parallelogram drawn from a common origin, their resultant vector is completely represented in magnitude and direction by the diagonal of the parallelogram passing through that same point.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Analytical Derivation:</b><br>\n      Let two vectors \u000bec{A} and \u000bec{B} be represented by sides \u000bec{OP} and \u000bec{OS} of parallelogram OPQS, with angle between them angle POS = \theta.<br>\n      The diagonal \u000bec{OQ} = \u000bec{R} represents the resultant. Drop perpendicular QN from Q onto extended line OP.<br>\n      In right triangle PNQ:<br>\n      PQ = OS = B, angle QPN = \theta.<br>\n      &bull; PN = PQ cos\theta = B cos\theta.<br>\n      &bull; QN = PQ sin\theta = B sin\theta.<br>\n      In right-angled triangle ONQ, applying Pythagoras theorem:<br>\n      OQ<sup>2</sup> = ON<sup>2</sup> + QN<sup>2</sup> = (OP + PN)<sup>2</sup> + QN<sup>2</sup><br>\n      R<sup>2</sup> = (A + Bcos\theta)<sup>2</sup> + (Bsin\theta)<sup>2</sup><br>\n      R<sup>2</sup> = A<sup>2</sup> + 2ABcos\theta + B<sup>2</sup>cos<sup>2</sup>\theta + B<sup>2</sup>sin<sup>2</sup>\theta = A<sup>2</sup> + B<sup>2</sup>(cos<sup>2</sup>\theta + sin<sup>2</sup>\theta) + 2ABcos\theta<br>\n      Since cos<sup>2</sup>\theta + sin<sup>2</sup>\theta = 1:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        R = sqrt{A<sup>2</sup> + B<sup>2</sup> + 2ABcos\theta}\n      </div>\n      <b>Direction of Resultant (&alpha; with vector A):</b><br>\n      In right triangle ONQ:<br>\n      \tanalpha = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">QN</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">ON</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">QN</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">OP + PN</span></span><br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        \tanalpha = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">Bsin\theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A + Bcos\theta</span></span> implies alpha = \tan<sup>-1</sup>left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">Bsin\theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A + Bcos\theta</span></span>\right)\n      </div>\n      <b>3. Special Cases:</b><br>\n      &bull; <b>Case I: \theta = 0<sup>c</sup>irc (Collinear in same direction):</b><br>\n      cos 0<sup>c</sup>irc = 1 implies R = sqrt{A<sup>2</sup> + B<sup>2</sup> + 2AB} = sqrt{(A+B)<sup>2</sup>} = mathbf{A + B} (Maximum resultant). \tanalpha = 0 implies alpha = 0<sup>c</sup>irc.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Case II: \theta = 180<sup>c</sup>irc (Opposite directions):</b><br>\n      cos 180<sup>c</sup>irc = -1 implies R = sqrt{A<sup>2</sup> + B<sup>2</sup> - 2AB} = sqrt{(A-B)<sup>2</sup>} = mathbf{|A - B|} (Minimum resultant). alpha = 0<sup>c</sup>irc (along larger vector).<br>\n      &bull; <b>Case III: \theta = 90<sup>c</sup>irc (Orthogonal vectors):</b><br>\n      cos 90<sup>c</sup>irc = 0, sin 90<sup>c</sup>irc = 1 implies mathbf{R = sqrt{A<sup>2</sup> + B<sup>2</sup>}} and \tanalpha = mathbf{B / A}.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q85: What is a projectile? A particle is projected with initial velocity u at an angle &theta; with the horizontal. Derive: (a) The equation of its trajectory and prove it is a parabola, (b) Time of maximum height and Total Time of Flight.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>Definition:</b> A projectile is any body thrown into space with an initial velocity, which thereafter moves under the sole influence of gravity without any propulsion engine.<br><br>\n      <b>Kinematic Decomposition:</b><br>\n      Initial velocity components:<br>\n      &bull; Horizontal: u_x = u cos\theta, Acceleration a_x = 0 (neglecting air resistance).<br>\n      &bull; Vertical: u_y = u sin\theta, Acceleration a_y = -g (directed downward).<br><br>\n      <b>(a) Equation of Trajectory:</b><br>\n      Horizontal displacement after time t:<br>\n      x = u_x t + \frac{1}{2}a_x t<sup>2</sup> = (u cos\theta)t implies mathbf{t = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">x</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u cos\theta</span></span>}.<br>\n      Vertical displacement after time t:<br>\n      y = u_y t - \frac{1}{2}gt<sup>2</sup> = (u sin\theta)t - \frac{1}{2}gt<sup>2</sup>.<br>\n      Substitute t = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">x</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u cos\theta</span></span> into the vertical equation:<br>\n      y = (u sin\theta)left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">x</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u cos\theta</span></span>\right) - \frac{1}{2}gleft(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">x</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u cos\theta</span></span>\right)<sup>2</sup><br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        y = x \tan\theta - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 u<sup>2</sup> cos<sup>2</sup>\theta</span></span> x<sup>2</sup>\n      </div>\n      This is of the standard mathematical form y = Ax - Bx<sup>2</sup> where A = \tan\theta and B = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2u<sup>2</sup>cos<sup>2</sup>\theta</span></span> are constants. Since this is a quadratic equation in x with a negative leading coefficient, <b>the trajectory of a projectile is strictly a parabola curving downward</b>.<br><br>\n      <b>(b) Time of Ascent and Total Time of Flight:</b><br>\n      At the peak of trajectory (H), the vertical component of velocity vanishes momentarily: v_y = 0.<br>\n      Using v_y = u_y - gt_a:<br>\n      0 = u sin\theta - gt_a implies mathbf{t_a = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">usin\theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>} (Time of Ascent).<br>\n      Because gravitational acceleration is symmetrical in uniform fields, time of ascent equals time of descent (t_a = t_d).<br>\n      Total Time of Flight T = t_a + t_d = 2t_a:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        T = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2 u sin\theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q86: For an oblique projectile launched at angle &theta; with speed u, derive expressions for: (a) Maximum height attained (H), (b) Horizontal Range (R). Hence prove the fundamental relation connecting Range and Maximum Height: R = 4H cot &theta;.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>(a) Maximum Height (H):</b><br>\n      At maximum height, vertical velocity v_y = 0.<br>\n      Using the kinematic equation v_y<sup>2</sup> = u_y<sup>2</sup> - 2gy with y = H and u_y = usin\theta:<br>\n      0 = (u sin\theta)<sup>2</sup> - 2gH implies 2gH = u<sup>2</sup> sin<sup>2</sup>\theta<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        H = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> sin<sup>2</sup>\theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2g</span></span>\n      </div>\n      <b>(b) Horizontal Range (R):</b><br>\n      Horizontal range is the total horizontal distance traversed during total time of flight T = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2usin\theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>.<br>\n      Since horizontal acceleration is zero (a_x = 0):<br>\n      R = u_x \times T = (u cos\theta) \times left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2usin\theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>\right) = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup>(2sin\thetacos\theta)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>.<br>\n      Applying the trigonometric identity 2sin\thetacos\theta = sin 2\theta:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        R = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> sin 2\theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>\n      </div>\n      <b>(c) Proof of R = 4H cot\theta:</b><br>\n      Divide the formula for R by H:<br>\n      <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">H</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> (2sin\thetacos\theta) / g</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> sin<sup>2</sup>\theta / (2g)</span></span><br>\n      Cancel u<sup>2</sup> / g from numerator and denominator:<br>\n      <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">H</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2sin\thetacos\theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">sin<sup>2</sup>\theta / 2</span></span> = 4 \times <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">cos\theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">sin\theta</span></span> = 4 cot\theta.<br>\n      Cross-multiplying gives:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        R = 4H cot\theta quad \text{or} quad \tan\theta = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4H</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span></span>\n      </div>\n      This is a cornerstone relation used to compute launch angle directly from peak height and range.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q87: Prove analytically that: (a) Horizontal range of a projectile is maximum when angle of projection is 45&deg;, (b) The horizontal range is identical for two angles of projection that are complementary (&theta; and 90&deg; - &theta;), (c) The product of times of flight for these two complementary angles satisfies T_1 &times; T_2 = 2R / g.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>(a) Condition for Maximum Range:</b><br>\n      Horizontal range is given by R = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> sin 2\theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>.<br>\n      For a given launch speed u and local gravity g, R is maximum when sin 2\theta reaches its maximum mathematical value, which is +1:<br>\n      sin 2\theta = 1 implies 2\theta = 90<sup>c</sup>irc implies mathbf{\theta = 45<sup>c</sup>irc}.<br>\n      Maximum horizontal range value:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        R_{max} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span> quad \text{and at this angle, } H = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R_{max}</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4</span></span>\n      </div>\n      <b>(b) Equal Range for Complementary Angles:</b><br>\n      Let the projectile be fired at angle \theta_1 = \theta:<br>\n      R_1 = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> sin(2\theta)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>.<br>\n      Now let the projectile be fired with identical speed at complementary angle \theta_2 = 90<sup>c</sup>irc - \theta:<br>\n      R_2 = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> sin[2(90<sup>c</sup>irc - \theta)]</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> sin(180<sup>c</sup>irc - 2\theta)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>.<br>\n      From trigonometry, sin(180<sup>c</sup>irc - phi) = sinphi, so sin(180<sup>c</sup>irc - 2\theta) = sin 2\theta.<br>\n      Therefore: R_2 = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup> sin 2\theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span> = R_1.<br>\n      <b>Hence, the horizontal range is strictly identical for complementary angles \theta and 90<sup>c</sup>irc - \theta (e.g. 30<sup>c</sup>irc and 60<sup>c</sup>irc).</b><br><br>\n      <b>(c) Product of Times of Flight (T_1 \times T_2):</b><br>\n      &bull; For angle \theta: T_1 = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2usin\theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>.<br>\n      &bull; For angle 90<sup>c</sup>irc - \theta: T_2 = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2usin(90<sup>c</sup>irc - \theta)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2ucos\theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>.<br>\n      Multiplying T_1 and T_2:<br>\n      T_1 T_2 = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2usin\theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>\right) \times left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2ucos\theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>\right) = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span> \times left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup>(2sin\thetacos\theta)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>\right).<br>\n      Recognizing that R = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup>(2sin\thetacos\theta)</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        T_1 T_2 = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2R</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q88: A projectile is launched with velocity u at an angle &theta; to the horizontal. Derive expressions for: (a) Velocity vector v, speed, and angle of motion &beta; at any instant t, (b) Velocity of projectile when it is at a vertical height h above ground.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>(a) Velocity Vector at Time t:</b><br>\n      At any elapsed time t, velocity components are:<br>\n      &bull; Horizontal component: v_x = u_x = u cos\theta (remains strictly constant).<br>\n      &bull; Vertical component: v_y = u_y - gt = u sin\theta - gt.<br>\n      In vector Cartesian form:<br>\n      \u000bec{v}(t) = v_x hat{i} + v_y hat{j} = (u cos\theta)hat{i} + (u sin\theta - gt)hat{j}.<br>\n      <b>Instantaneous Speed:</b><br>\n      v = |\u000bec{v}| = sqrt{v_x<sup>2</sup> + v_y<sup>2</sup>} = sqrt{(ucos\theta)<sup>2</sup> + (usin\theta - gt)<sup>2</sup>}<br>\n      v = sqrt{u<sup>2</sup>cos<sup>2</sup>\theta + u<sup>2</sup>sin<sup>2</sup>\theta - 2u gtsin\theta + g<sup>2</sup> t<sup>2</sup>} = mathbf{sqrt{u<sup>2</sup> - 2ugtsin\theta + g<sup>2</sup> t<sup>2</sup>}}.<br>\n      <b>Direction (\beta with horizontal):</b><br>\n      \tan\beta = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v_y</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v_x</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">usin\theta - gt</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">ucos\theta</span></span> implies mathbf{\beta = \tan<sup>-1</sup>left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">usin\theta - gt</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">ucos\theta</span></span>\right)}.<br><br>\n      <b>(b) Velocity at Height h:</b><br>\n      Using the third kinematic equation for vertical motion: v_y<sup>2</sup> = u_y<sup>2</sup> - 2gh = (usin\theta)<sup>2</sup> - 2gh.<br>\n      Horizontal velocity remains: v_x<sup>2</sup> = (ucos\theta)<sup>2</sup>.<br>\n      Total speed at height h is:<br>\n      v = sqrt{v_x<sup>2</sup> + v_y<sup>2</sup>} = sqrt{(ucos\theta)<sup>2</sup> + (usin\theta)<sup>2</sup> - 2gh} = sqrt{u<sup>2</sup>(cos<sup>2</sup>\theta + sin<sup>2</sup>\theta) - 2gh}.<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        v = sqrt{u<sup>2</sup> - 2gh}\n      </div>\n      <b>Physical Insight:</b> This result is completely independent of launch angle \theta and directly proves conservation of mechanical energy: \frac{1}{2}m v<sup>2</sup> + mgh = \frac{1}{2}m u<sup>2</sup>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q89: A body is projected horizontally with a velocity u from the top of a cliff of height h. Derive: (a) Equation of its trajectory, (b) Time taken to strike the ground, (c) Horizontal distance from base of cliff (Range), (d) Resultant velocity on striking ground.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>Initial Conditions:</b><br>\n      At the cliff edge (origin O):<br>\n      &bull; Initial horizontal velocity: u_x = u, acceleration a_x = 0.<br>\n      &bull; Initial vertical velocity: u_y = 0, acceleration a_y = +g (taking downward direction as positive).<br><br>\n      <b>(a) Trajectory Equation:</b><br>\n      Horizontal distance after time t: x = u cdot t implies t = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">x</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u</span></span>.<br>\n      Vertical downward displacement: y = \frac{1}{2}gt<sup>2</sup>.<br>\n      Substitute t = x/u:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        y = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2u<sup>2</sup></span></span>\right) x<sup>2</sup> = k x<sup>2</sup>\n      </div>\n      This is the equation of a vertical parabola having vertex at origin O.<br><br>\n      <b>(b) Time Taken to Strike Ground (T):</b><br>\n      When the body reaches the ground, downward vertical displacement y = h.<br>\n      h = \frac{1}{2}g T<sup>2</sup> implies T<sup>2</sup> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2h</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span> implies mathbf{T = sqrt{<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2h</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>}}.<br>\n      Notice that this time is identical to the time taken by a body simply dropped vertically from rest from the same height.<br><br>\n      <b>(c) Horizontal Range (R):</b><br>\n      Horizontal range is the distance from base to impact point:<br>\n      R = u_x \times T = mathbf{u sqrt{<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2h</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>}}.<br><br>\n      <b>(d) Velocity upon Striking Ground:</b><br>\n      &bull; Horizontal velocity: v_x = u.<br>\n      &bull; Vertical velocity: v_y<sup>2</sup> = 0 + 2gh implies v_y = sqrt{2gh}.<br>\n      Resultant impact speed:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        v = sqrt{v_x<sup>2</sup> + v_y<sup>2</sup>} = sqrt{u<sup>2</sup> + 2gh}\n      </div>\n      Impact angle \beta with the ground horizontal: \tan\beta = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v_y</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v_x</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">sqrt{2gh}</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u</span></span>.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q90: Define Uniform Circular Motion. Derive the expression for Centripetal Acceleration (a_c = v&sup2; / r = &omega;&sup2; r) using vector calculus / similar triangles. Explain why centripetal force does zero work on the particle.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Definition:</b> When a particle traverses a circular track of radius r at constant speed v, its motion is called <b>Uniform Circular Motion (UCM)</b>. Although speed is constant, the direction of velocity changes continuously, creating acceleration directed radially toward the center.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Derivation using Vector Geometry:</b><br>\n      Let a particle move around a circle of radius r centered at O.<br>\n      At time t, position vector is \u000bec{r}_1 (point P_1) with velocity \u000bec{v}_1.<br>\n      At time t + Delta t, position vector is \u000bec{r}_2 (point P_2) with velocity \u000bec{v}_2.<br>\n      Angle subtended: angle P_1 O P_2 = Delta\theta.<br>\n      Position displacement: Delta\u000bec{r} = \u000bec{r}_2 - \u000bec{r}_1.<br>\n      Velocity change: Delta\u000bec{v} = \u000bec{v}_2 - \u000bec{v}_1.<br><br>\n      Construct a velocity triangle with vectors \u000bec{v}_1, \u000bec{v}_2 and Delta\u000bec{v}.<br>\n      Since \u000bec{v}_1 perp \u000bec{r}_1 and \u000bec{v}_2 perp \u000bec{r}_2, the angle between \u000bec{v}_1 and \u000bec{v}_2 is also Delta\theta.<br>\n      Both triangles Delta O P_1 P_2 and the velocity triangle are isosceles with common vertex angle Delta\theta, making them <b>geometrically similar</b>:<br>\n      <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">|Delta\u000bec{v}|</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">|Delta\u000bec{r}|</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span></span> implies |Delta\u000bec{v}| = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span></span> |Delta\u000bec{r}|.<br>\n      Divide both sides by elapsed time Delta t and take the limit Delta t \to 0:<br>\n      lim_{Delta t \to 0} <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">|Delta\u000bec{v}|</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">Delta t</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span></span> left(lim_{Delta t \to 0} <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">|Delta\u000bec{r}|</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">Delta t</span></span>\right).<br>\n      By definition, instantaneous acceleration is a_c = lim_{Delta t \to 0} \frac{|Delta\u000bec{v}|}{Delta t}, and instantaneous speed is v = lim_{Delta t \to 0} \frac{|Delta\u000bec{r}|}{Delta t}.<br>\n      Substituting gives:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        a_c = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span></span>\n      </div>\n      Using linear-angular relation v = romega:<br>\n      a_c = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">(romega)<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span></span> = mathbf{omega<sup>2</sup> r} = mathbf{4pi<sup>2</sup> \nu<sup>2</sup> r}.<br>\n      As Delta t \to 0, vector Delta\u000bec{v} points perpendicular to the velocity vector, directed inwards towards center O.<br><br>\n      <b>3. Work Done by Centripetal Force:</b><br>\n      Work done is W = \u000bec{F}_c cdot Delta\u000bec{r} = F_c , Delta r cos\theta.<br>\n      At every instant, centripetal force \u000bec{F}_c acts along the radius toward the center, while displacement Delta\u000bec{r} acts along the tangent.<br>\n      Angle \theta = 90<sup>c</sup>irc implies cos 90<sup>c</sup>irc = 0.<br>\n      <b>W = F_c , Delta r cos 90<sup>c</sup>irc = 0. Centripetal force performs strictly zero work.</b>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q91: Establish the mathematical relations connecting linear and angular kinematic variables: (a) Linear displacement and angular displacement (s = r&theta;), (b) Linear velocity and angular velocity (v = &omega; &times; r), (c) Linear tangential acceleration and angular acceleration (a_t = &alpha; &times; r).</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>(a) Linear Displacement and Angular Displacement (s = r\theta):</b><br>\n      By circular geometry, angle subtended in radians is defined as ratio of arc length to radius:<br>\n      \theta = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">s</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span></span> implies mathbf{s = r\theta}.<br>\n      For an infinitesimal displacement: ds = r , d\theta.<br><br>\n      <b>(b) Linear Velocity and Angular Velocity (\u000bec{v} = \u000bec{omega}\times\u000bec{r}):</b><br>\n      Differentiating s = r\theta with respect to time t for a fixed radius r:<br>\n      <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">ds</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> = r , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d\theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span>.<br>\n      Since linear speed v = ds/dt and angular speed omega = d\theta/dt:<br>\n      mathbf{v = romega}.<br>\n      <b>Vector Cross Product Form:</b><br>\n      Angular velocity \u000bec{omega} is an axial vector directed along the axis of rotation (via Right Hand Thumb Rule). The linear velocity vector \u000bec{v} is given by:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        \u000bec{v} = \u000bec{omega} \times \u000bec{r}\n      </div>\n      Magnitude: |\u000bec{v}| = omega r sin 90<sup>c</sup>irc = omega r. Direction: Tangential, perpendicular to both \u000bec{omega} and \u000bec{r}.<br><br>\n      <b>(c) Tangential Acceleration and Angular Acceleration (\u000bec{a}_t = \u000bec{alpha}\times\u000bec{r}):</b><br>\n      Differentiating the vector velocity expression with respect to time:<br>\n      <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d\u000bec{v}</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span>(\u000bec{omega} \times \u000bec{r}) = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d\u000bec{omega}</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> \times \u000bec{r}\right) + left(\u000bec{omega} \times <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d\u000bec{r}</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span>\right).<br>\n      Recognizing that \u000bec{alpha} = \frac{d\u000bec{omega}}{dt} (angular acceleration) and \frac{d\u000bec{r}}{dt} = \u000bec{v}:<br>\n      \u000bec{a}_{\text{net}} = (\u000bec{alpha} \times \u000bec{r}) + (\u000bec{omega} \times \u000bec{v}).<br>\n      &bull; The first term \u000bec{a}_t = \u000bec{alpha} \times \u000bec{r} acts tangentially and changes speed magnitude: mathbf{a_t = ralpha}.<br>\n      &bull; The second term \u000bec{a}_c = \u000bec{omega} \times \u000bec{v} acts centripetally toward center and changes direction: mathbf{a_c = omega v = omega<sup>2</sup> r = v<sup>2</sup>/r}.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q92: Explain relative velocity in two dimensions. Formulate the Rain-Man Problem: A person walks with velocity v_m while rain falls vertically with velocity v_r. Derive the magnitude and angle at which an umbrella must be tilted. Analyze what happens if wind starts blowing.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Conceptual Foundation:</b><br>\n      Relative velocity of an object A observed from the frame of reference of object B is defined as:<br>\n      \u000bec{v}_{AB} = \u000bec{v}_A - \u000bec{v}_B = \u000bec{v}_A + (-\u000bec{v}_B).<br>\n      To find the velocity of A relative to B, superimpose a velocity equal and opposite to B's velocity onto both objects, bringing observer B to virtual rest.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Rain-Man Problem Formulation:</b><br>\n      Let vertical downward direction be -hat{j} and horizontal forward direction be +hat{i}.<br>\n      &bull; Velocity of rain: \u000bec{v}_r = -v_r hat{j}.<br>\n      &bull; Velocity of moving person: \u000bec{v}_m = v_m hat{i}.<br>\n      The velocity of rain relative to the person is:<br>\n      \u000bec{v}_{rm} = \u000bec{v}_r - \u000bec{v}_m = -v_r hat{j} - v_m hat{i} = -v_m hat{i} - v_r hat{j}.<br><br>\n      <b>Magnitude of Relative Velocity:</b><br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        v_{rm} = sqrt{v_r<sup>2</sup> + v_m<sup>2</sup>}\n      </div>\n      <b>Angle of Umbrella Tilt (&theta; with vertical):</b><br>\n      To protect against rain falling along \u000bec{v}_{rm}, the umbrella must be held in the exact opposite direction (-\u000bec{v}_{rm} = v_m hat{i} + v_r hat{j}), tilted forward toward the direction of motion at an angle \theta with the vertical:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        \tan\theta = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v_m</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v_r</span></span> implies \theta = \tan<sup>-1</sup>left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v_m</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v_r</span></span>\right)\n      </div>\n      <b>3. Effect of Horizontal Wind (\u000bec{v}_w = v_w hat{i}):</b><br>\n      If wind blows horizontally, true velocity of rain becomes \u000bec{v}_r = v_w hat{i} - v_r hat{j}.<br>\n      Relative velocity to moving person:<br>\n      \u000bec{v}_{rm} = \u000bec{v}_r - \u000bec{v}_m = (v_w - v_m)hat{i} - v_r hat{j}.<br>\n      Umbrella angle with vertical:<br>\n      \tan\theta' = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">|v_w - v_m|</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v_r</span></span>.<br>\n      If v_m = v_w, the relative horizontal velocity vanishes: \u000bec{v}_{rm} = -v_r hat{j}, meaning the person feels the rain falling purely vertically and must hold the umbrella straight upright!\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q93: Analyze the River-Boat Crossing Problem in two dimensions: (a) Derive the condition, steering angle, and time taken to cross the river along the shortest path (directly across, zero drift), (b) Derive the condition and time taken to cross in minimum time, and calculate the resulting downstream drift.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>Setup:</b><br>\n      Let river width be d, river flow velocity be \u000bec{v}_r = v_r hat{i} (downstream along x-axis).<br>\n      Let speed of boat in still water be v_b. Boat's engine directs it at an angle \theta to the vertical y-axis (upstream direction).<br><br>\n      <b>(a) Shortest Path Crossing (Zero Drift, x = 0):</b><br>\n      To land directly opposite on the bank (along y-axis), the resultant horizontal velocity must be zero:<br>\n      v_{net, x} = v_r - v_b sin\theta = 0 implies mathbf{sin\theta = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v_r</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v_b</span></span>}.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Feasibility Condition:</b> Since sin\theta le 1, zero drift is physically possible only if <b>v_b > v_r</b> (boat speed exceeds river speed).<br>\n      &bull; Resultant crossing velocity across the river:<br>\n      v_{net, y} = v_b cos\theta = v_b sqrt{1 - sin<sup>2</sup>\theta} = sqrt{v_b<sup>2</sup> - v_r<sup>2</sup>}.<br>\n      &bull; Time taken for shortest path:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        t_{\text{shortest path}} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">sqrt{v_b<sup>2</sup> - v_r<sup>2</sup>}</span></span>\n      </div>\n      <b>(b) Minimum Time Crossing:</b><br>\n      Time to cross depends solely on vertical velocity component perpendicular to the bank: t = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v_b cos\theta</span></span>.<br>\n      Time t is minimum when the denominator is maximized, which occurs when cos\theta = 1 implies mathbf{\theta = 0<sup>c</sup>irc}.<br>\n      The boat must steer <b>strictly perpendicular to the river bank</b>.<br>\n      &bull; Minimum crossing time:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        t_{min} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v_b</span></span>\n      </div>\n      &bull; Resulting downstream drift (x):<br>\n      While crossing, river current pushes the boat horizontally:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        \text{Drift } x = v_r \times t_{min} = v_r left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v_b</span></span>\right) = d left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v_r</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v_b</span></span>\right)\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q94: State the definition of the Scalar (Dot) Product of two vectors. Enumerate its six fundamental mathematical properties, explain its geometrical interpretation as an orthogonal projection, and derive the formula for work done by a variable or constant force vector.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Definition:</b><br>\n      The scalar or dot product of two vectors \u000bec{A} and \u000bec{B} inclined at an angle \theta (0 le \theta le pi) is defined as the product of their magnitudes and the cosine of the included angle:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        \u000bec{A} cdot \u000bec{B} = A B cos\theta\n      </div>\n      The result is a pure scalar quantity.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Six Fundamental Properties:</b><br>\n      1. <b>Commutative Property:</b> \u000bec{A} cdot \u000bec{B} = \u000bec{B} cdot \u000bec{A} (since cos\theta = cos(-\theta)).<br>\n      2. <b>Distributive Property:</b> \u000bec{A} cdot (\u000bec{B} + \u000bec{C}) = \u000bec{A} cdot \u000bec{B} + \u000bec{A} cdot \u000bec{C}.<br>\n      3. <b>Self Dot Product:</b> \u000bec{A} cdot \u000bec{A} = A cdot A cos 0<sup>c</sup>irc = A<sup>2</sup> implies A = sqrt{\u000bec{A} cdot \u000bec{A}}.<br>\n      4. <b>Orthogonal Vectors (\theta = 90<sup>c</sup>irc):</b> \u000bec{A} cdot \u000bec{B} = A B cos 90<sup>c</sup>irc = 0. Mutual orthogonality test: \u000bec{A} cdot \u000bec{B} = 0.<br>\n      5. <b>Unit Vector Orthogonality:</b><br>\n      &bull; hat{i} cdot hat{i} = hat{j} cdot hat{j} = hat{k} cdot hat{k} = 1<br>\n      &bull; hat{i} cdot hat{j} = hat{j} cdot hat{k} = hat{k} cdot hat{i} = 0.<br>\n      6. <b>Cartesian Component Formula:</b> For \u000bec{A} = A_xhat{i} + A_yhat{j} + A_zhat{k} and \u000bec{B} = B_xhat{i} + B_yhat{j} + B_zhat{k}:<br>\n      \u000bec{A} cdot \u000bec{B} = A_x B_x + A_y B_y + A_z B_z.<br><br>\n      <b>3. Geometrical Interpretation:</b><br>\n      \u000bec{A} cdot \u000bec{B} = A(Bcos\theta) = (\text{Magnitude of } \u000bec{A}) \times (\text{Scalar projection of } \u000bec{B} \text{ onto } \u000bec{A}).<br>\n      Projection of \u000bec{B} on \u000bec{A} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">\u000bec{A} cdot \u000bec{B}</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A</span></span>.<br><br>\n      <b>4. Application to Work Done:</b><br>\n      Mechanical work done W by a constant force vector \u000bec{F} causing displacement Delta\u000bec{r} = Delta xhat{i} + Delta yhat{j} + Delta zhat{k} is:<br>\n      W = \u000bec{F} cdot Delta\u000bec{r} = F_x Delta x + F_y Delta y + F_z Delta z.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q95: State the definition of the Vector (Cross) Product of two vectors. Enumerate its six core properties, explain the Right Hand Thumb Rule, prove that the area of a parallelogram equals |A &times; B|, and express torque &tau; = r &times; F in 3x3 determinant form.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Definition:</b><br>\n      The vector or cross product of two vectors \u000bec{A} and \u000bec{B} inclined at angle \theta is a vector \u000bec{C} defined as:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        \u000bec{A} \times \u000bec{B} = (A B sin\theta) hat{n}\n      </div>\n      where hat{n} is a unit vector perpendicular to the plane containing \u000bec{A} and \u000bec{B}, oriented according to the Right-Hand Thumb Rule.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Six Core Properties:</b><br>\n      1. <b>Anti-Commutative:</b> \u000bec{A} \times \u000bec{B} = -(\u000bec{B} \times \u000bec{A}).<br>\n      2. <b>Distributive:</b> \u000bec{A} \times (\u000bec{B} + \u000bec{C}) = (\u000bec{A} \times \u000bec{B}) + (\u000bec{A} \times \u000bec{C}).<br>\n      3. <b>Collinear Vectors (\theta = 0<sup>c</sup>irc or 180<sup>c</sup>irc):</b> \u000bec{A} \times \u000bec{B} = \u000bec{0} (null vector). Collinearity test: \u000bec{A} \times \u000bec{B} = 0.<br>\n      4. <b>Self Cross Product:</b> \u000bec{A} \times \u000bec{A} = \u000bec{0}.<br>\n      5. <b>Unit Vector Orthogonal Relations:</b><br>\n      &bull; hat{i} \times hat{i} = hat{j} \times hat{j} = hat{k} \times hat{k} = \u000bec{0}<br>\n      &bull; Cyclic: hat{i} \times hat{j} = hat{k}, hat{j} \times hat{k} = hat{i}, hat{k} \times hat{i} = hat{j}<br>\n      &bull; Anti-cyclic: hat{j} \times hat{i} = -hat{k}, hat{k} \times hat{j} = -hat{i}, hat{i} \times hat{k} = -hat{j}.<br>\n      6. <b>Determinant Form:</b><br>\n      \u000bec{A} \times \u000bec{B} = \begin{vmatrix} hat{i} & hat{j} & hat{k} \\ A_x & A_y & A_z \\ B_x & B_y & B_z end{vmatrix} = hat{i}(A_y B_z - A_z B_y) - hat{j}(A_x B_z - A_z B_x) + hat{k}(A_x B_y - A_y B_x).<br><br>\n      <b>3. Area of Parallelogram:</b><br>\n      Consider parallelogram OPQS spanned by adjacent vector sides \u000bec{OP} = \u000bec{A} and \u000bec{OS} = \u000bec{B} with angle \theta.<br>\n      \text{Area} = \text{base} \times \text{height} = OP \times (OS sin\theta) = A B sin\theta = |\u000bec{A} \times \u000bec{B}|.<br>\n      Similarly, Area of Delta OPQ = \frac{1}{2} |\u000bec{A} \times \u000bec{B}|.<br><br>\n      <b>4. Torque Application:</b><br>\n      Torque \u000bec{\tau} = \u000bec{r} \times \u000bec{F} = \begin{vmatrix} hat{i} & hat{j} & hat{k} \\ x & y & z \\ F_x & F_y & F_z end{vmatrix}.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q96: Explain non-uniform circular motion in detail. Distinguish between tangential acceleration (a_t) and centripetal acceleration (a_c). Derive the expression for net acceleration vector a, its magnitude a = &radic;(a_t&sup2; + a_c&sup2;), and the angle &phi; it makes with the radius vector.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Conceptual Foundation:</b><br>\n      In <b>Non-Uniform Circular Motion</b>, a particle moves along a circular path of radius r with variable speed v(t). Because both the speed (magnitude) and direction of the velocity vector change simultaneously with time, the motion involves two distinct, mutually orthogonal components of acceleration.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Two Acceleration Components:</b><br>\n      &bull; <b>Centripetal (Radial) Acceleration (a_c):</b> Acts radially inward along the radius vector toward the center of curvature. It is responsible solely for changing the spatial direction of the velocity vector:<br>\n      a_c = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span></span> = omega<sup>2</sup> r.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Tangential Acceleration (a_t):</b> Acts along the tangent to the circular path (collinear with instantaneous velocity). It is responsible solely for changing the scalar speed of the particle:<br>\n      a_t = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dv</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> = r alpha quad \text{where } alpha = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">domega</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span>.<br><br>\n      <b>3. Net Acceleration Vector:</b><br>\n      In polar coordinates, net acceleration vector \u000bec{a} is the vector sum of radial and tangential components:<br>\n      \u000bec{a} = \u000bec{a}_c + \u000bec{a}_t = -a_c hat{r} + a_t hat{\theta}.<br>\n      Since radial and tangential directions are strictly perpendicular (angle = 90<sup>c</sup>irc), applying Pythagoras theorem:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        a = |\u000bec{a}| = sqrt{a_c<sup>2</sup> + a_t<sup>2</sup>} = sqrt{left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span></span>\right)<sup>2</sup> + left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dv</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span>\right)<sup>2</sup>} = sqrt{(omega<sup>2</sup> r)<sup>2</sup> + (ralpha)<sup>2</sup>}\n      </div>\n      <b>4. Direction of Net Acceleration:</b><br>\n      Let phi be the angle that the net acceleration vector \u000bec{a} makes with the inward radius vector:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 8px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        \tanphi = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a_t</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a_c</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">ralpha</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v<sup>2</sup> / r</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r<sup>2</sup> alpha</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v<sup>2</sup></span></span> implies phi = \tan<sup>-1</sup>left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a_t</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">a_c</span></span>\right)\n      </div>\n      &bull; If speed is constant (dv/dt = 0), a_t = 0 implies phi = 0<sup>c</sup>irc (pure UCM, net acceleration is purely radial).<br>\n      &bull; If radius r \to infty (straight line), a_c = 0 implies phi = 90<sup>c</sup>irc (rectilinear acceleration).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q97: Discuss Velocity-Time (v-t) graphs in depth: Show how slope represents acceleration and area represents displacement. Sketch and analytically analyze: (a) A ball thrown vertically upwards and caught upon return, (b) A ball dropped from height h undergoing elastic bounce on the floor, (c) Motion with constant positive acceleration vs increasing acceleration.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Mathematical Principles of the v-t Graph:</b><br>\n      &bull; <b>Slope:</b> By definition, a = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dv</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span>. Hence, the slope of the tangent at any point on the v-t curve gives the instantaneous acceleration.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Area under Curve:</b> Since ds = v , dt, total displacement is s = int v , dt. The definite integral represents the geometric area under the v-t graph bounded by the time axis. Area above the time axis represents positive displacement; area below represents negative displacement.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Physical Motion Profiles:</b><br>\n      <b>(a) Ball Thrown Vertically Upward & Caught:</b><br>\n      Taking upward as positive:<br>\n      &bull; At t = 0, v = +u.<br>\n      &bull; Throughout the flight, acceleration is constant downward: a = -g = -9.8\text{ m/s}<sup>2</sup>.<br>\n      &bull; The v-t graph is a <b>straight line with constant negative slope (-g)</b> crossing the time axis at peak time t = u/g (v = 0), and reaching v = -u at total time T = 2u/g.<br>\n      &bull; Area of positive triangle (ascent) = +H; Area of negative triangle (descent) = -H. Net displacement = H - H = 0. Total distance = H + H = 2H.<br><br>\n      <b>(b) Ball Dropped and Undergoing Elastic Bounce:</b><br>\n      Taking downward as positive:<br>\n      &bull; Starts from rest (v = 0 at t = 0), accelerates downward with slope +g until hitting floor at t_1 = sqrt{2h/g} with velocity v_1 = +sqrt{2gh}.<br>\n      &bull; At impact, velocity reverses instantaneously from +sqrt{2gh} to -sqrt{2gh} (represented by a vertical discontinuity line).<br>\n      &bull; Moves upward under gravity with slope +g from v = -sqrt{2gh} to v = 0 at peak height.<br><br>\n      <b>(c) Constant vs Increasing Acceleration:</b><br>\n      &bull; <b>Constant a:</b> Straight line with uniform slope \tan\theta = a.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Increasing a (da/dt > 0):</b> Convex curve bending upwards with continuously steepening slope.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q98: A car accelerates from rest at a constant rate &alpha; for time t_1, and then decelerates at a constant rate &beta; to come to rest in total elapsed time T. Derive expressions for: (a) Maximum velocity attained (v_max), (b) Total distance traversed (S) in terms of &alpha;, &beta;, and T.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Breakdown into Two Motion Phases:</b><br>\n      Let total time be T = t_1 + t_2, where t_1 is acceleration duration and t_2 is retardation duration.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Phase 1 (Acceleration from rest):</b> Initial velocity u = 0, acceleration = alpha.<br>\n      Maximum velocity reached at t = t_1 is:<br>\n      v_{max} = 0 + alpha t_1 implies mathbf{t_1 = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v_{max}</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">alpha</span></span>}.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Phase 2 (Deceleration to rest):</b> Initial velocity = v_{max}, final velocity = 0, deceleration = \beta.<br>\n      0 = v_{max} - \beta t_2 implies mathbf{t_2 = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v_{max}</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">\beta</span></span>}.<br><br>\n      <b>(a) Maximum Velocity (v_{max}):</b><br>\n      Summing the two time intervals:<br>\n      T = t_1 + t_2 = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v_{max}</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">alpha</span></span> + <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v_{max}</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">\beta</span></span> = v_{max} left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">alpha + \beta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">alpha \beta</span></span>\right).<br>\n      Solving for v_{max}:<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        v_{max} = left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">alpha \beta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">alpha + \beta</span></span>\right) T\n      </div>\n      <b>(b) Total Distance Traversed (S):</b><br>\n      On the velocity-time graph, this motion forms a triangle with base equal to total time T and height equal to v_{max}.<br>\n      Total displacement is the area of this triangle:<br>\n      S = \frac{1}{2} \times \text{base} \times \text{height} = \frac{1}{2} \times T \times v_{max}.<br>\n      Substitute the expression for v_{max}:<br>\n      S = \frac{1}{2} T \times left[ left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">alpha \beta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">alpha + \beta</span></span>\right) T \right]<br>\n      <div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 10px 0; color: #76FF03; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold;\">\n        S = \frac{1}{2} left(<span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">alpha \beta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">alpha + \beta</span></span>\right) T<sup>2</sup>\n      </div>\n      This elegant result demonstrates how two distinct kinematic phases collapse into a single harmonic-mean acceleration form.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q99: Two particles are projected simultaneously from two different points in a vertical plane. Derive the condition for their mid-air collision. Prove analytically that the trajectory of one projectile as viewed from the other is a straight line.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Proof that Relative Trajectory is a Straight Line:</b><br>\n      Let particle 1 be launched from origin (0,0) with initial velocity \u000bec{u}_1 = u_{1x}hat{i} + u_{1y}hat{j}.<br>\n      Position vector of particle 1 at time t is:<br>\n      \u000bec{r}_1(t) = (u_{1x} t)hat{i} + left(u_{1y} t - \frac{1}{2}gt<sup>2</sup>\right)hat{j}.<br>\n      Let particle 2 be launched from point (x_0, y_0) with initial velocity \u000bec{u}_2 = u_{2x}hat{i} + u_{2y}hat{j}.<br>\n      Position vector of particle 2 at time t is:<br>\n      \u000bec{r}_2(t) = (x_0 + u_{2x} t)hat{i} + left(y_0 + u_{2y} t - \frac{1}{2}gt<sup>2</sup>\right)hat{j}.<br><br>\n      Relative position vector of particle 1 with respect to particle 2 is:<br>\n      \u000bec{r}_{12}(t) = \u000bec{r}_1(t) - \u000bec{r}_2(t) = left[(u_{1x} - u_{2x})t - x_0\right]hat{i} + left[(u_{1y} - u_{2y})t - y_0\right]hat{j}.<br>\n      Notice that the non-linear -\frac{1}{2}gt<sup>2</sup> gravitational term cancels out completely because both particles experience identical downward acceleration \u000bec{g}!<br>\n      Relative acceleration is identically zero: \u000bec{a}_{12} = \u000bec{g} - \u000bec{g} = \u000bec{0}.<br>\n      The coordinates of relative position satisfy:<br>\n      x_{12} = (u_{1x} - u_{2x})t - x_0 implies t = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">x_{12} + x_0</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u_{1x} - u_{2x}</span></span>.<br>\n      Substituting t into y_{12} yields a linear equation of the form y_{12} = m x_{12} + c.<br>\n      <b>Hence, the path of one projectile as observed by another projectile is strictly a straight line.</b><br><br>\n      <b>2. Condition for Mid-Air Collision:</b><br>\n      For collision to occur at time t_c, both particles must occupy the exact same spatial location: \u000bec{r}_1(t_c) = \u000bec{r}_2(t_c), which implies \u000bec{r}_{12}(t_c) = \u000bec{0}.<br>\n      This requires the relative velocity vector \u000bec{u}_{12} = \u000bec{u}_1 - \u000bec{u}_2 to be directed straight along the line joining their initial launch positions: \u000bec{r}_2(0) - \u000bec{r}_1(0).\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: rgba(0,0,0,0.2); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px; margin-bottom: 20px;\">\n    <h3 style=\"color: #8E2DE2; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 17px; font-weight: bold; line-height: 1.5;\">Q100: Master Synthesis of Unit II: Kinematics (09 Marks). Provide a comprehensive, rigorous conceptual review mapping 1D rectilinear mechanics, calculus operators, 2D vector kinematics, projectile motion invariants, and circular acceleration constraints for CBSE Board Examinations.</h3>\n    <div style=\"background: rgba(142, 45, 226, 0.05); border-left: 3.5px solid #8E2DE2; padding: 12px 14px; border-radius: 4px; color: #E0E0E0; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 15px;\">\n      <b style=\"color: #8E2DE2; display: block; margin-bottom: 6px;\">💡 Complete Step-by-Step Derivation & Theoretical Proof (5 Marks):</b>\n      <b>1. Comprehensive Conceptual Map of Kinematics:</b><br>\n      Unit II: Kinematics (09 Marks) describes physical motion geometrically without reference to the forces causing it.<br><br>\n      <b>2. Dimensional & Operational Matrix:</b><br>\n      &bull; <b>Calculus Operators:</b><br>\n      v = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dx</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> quad Longleftrightarrow quad x = int v , dt<br>\n      a = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dv</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt</span></span> = v , <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dv</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dx</span></span> = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">d<sup>2</sup>x</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">dt<sup>2</sup></span></span> quad Longleftrightarrow quad v = int a , dt.<br>\n      &bull; <b>Uniformly Accelerated Rectilinear Motion (a = \text{const}):</b><br>\n      1. v = u + at<br>\n      2. s = ut + \frac{1}{2}at<sup>2</sup><br>\n      3. v<sup>2</sup> - u<sup>2</sup> = 2as<br>\n      4. s_n = u + \frac{a}{2}(2n - 1).<br><br>\n      <b>3. Vector Algebra in 2D Plane:</b><br>\n      &bull; Resultant: R = sqrt{A<sup>2</sup> + B<sup>2</sup> + 2ABcos\theta}, \tanalpha = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">Bsin\theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">A + Bcos\theta</span></span>.<br>\n      &bull; Dot Product: \u000bec{A} cdot \u000bec{B} = ABcos\theta = A_x B_x + A_y B_y + A_z B_z. (W = \u000bec{F} cdot Delta\u000bec{r}).<br>\n      &bull; Cross Product: \u000bec{A} \times \u000bec{B} = (ABsin\theta)hat{n} = \begin{vmatrix}hat{i}&hat{j}&hat{k}\\A_x&A_y&A_z\\B_x&B_y&B_zend{vmatrix}. (\u000bec{\tau} = \u000bec{r} \times \u000bec{F}).<br><br>\n      <b>4. Projectile Motion Core Invariants:</b><br>\n      &bull; Trajectory: y = x\tan\theta - <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2u<sup>2</sup>cos<sup>2</sup>\theta</span></span> x<sup>2</sup> (Parabolic).<br>\n      &bull; Time of Flight: T = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2usin\theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>.<br>\n      &bull; Maximum Height: H = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup>sin<sup>2</sup>\theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">2g</span></span>.<br>\n      &bull; Horizontal Range: R = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup>sin 2\theta</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span>; R_{max} = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">u<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">g</span></span> at \theta = 45<sup>c</sup>irc.<br>\n      &bull; Master Relation: R = 4H cot\theta implies \tan\theta = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">4H</span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">R</span></span>.<br>\n      &bull; Invariant: Horizontal velocity v_x = ucos\theta is conserved throughout flight.<br><br>\n      <b>5. Circular Motion Dynamics:</b><br>\n      &bull; Angular-Linear: s = r\theta, v = romega, a_t = ralpha.<br>\n      &bull; Centripetal Acceleration: a_c = <span style=\"display: inline-flex; flex-direction: column; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; font-size: 0.88em; line-height: 1; margin: 0 3px;\"><span style=\"border-bottom: 1.5px solid currentColor; padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">v<sup>2</sup></span><span style=\"padding: 0 2px; display: inline-block;\">r</span></span> = omega<sup>2</sup> r = 4pi<sup>2</sup>\nu<sup>2</sup> r.<br>\n      &bull; Non-Uniform Circular: Net a = sqrt{a_c<sup>2</sup> + a_t<sup>2</sup>}, \tanphi = a_t / a_c.<br><br>\n      <b>6. Top 5 Board Examination Trap Alerts:</b><br>\n      1. Never apply v = u + at when acceleration varies with time or position (must integrate!).<br>\n      2. At the peak of projectile flight, acceleration is NOT zero; it is strictly g downward.<br>\n      3. Centripetal force performs ZERO work because \u000bec{F}_c perp \u000bec{v} at every instant.<br>\n      4. A body can have zero velocity with non-zero acceleration (e.g. ball at highest point of vertical toss).<br>\n      5. Caret symbols ($) are strictly forbidden in final presentation; standard superscripts must be used.\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n</div>";

